// ======================================================================
// Build stamp. Shown on the sign-in screen and in the footer so you can
// tell at a glance whether the deployed site is the version you pushed —
// GitHub Pages and the browser cache can both lag a commit behind.
//
// `build` matches the ?v= cache-busting number on every asset URL in
// index.html; bump both together when releasing.
//
// TUNO uses the SAME two-series idea as ENCA (see its js/version.js for the
// full rationale): production builds are plain integers on `main`; beta
// builds live in their own series that can never collide, and `cycle` IS NOT
// DERIVED FROM `build` — set it once per cycle, when the first beta build
// after a production release is cut.
//
// ONE DELIBERATE DIFFERENCE FROM ENCA. Its "five digits NNNII" rule only
// works because ENCA's production numbers are in the hundreds — TUNO starts
// at build 1, where cycle 2 iteration 1 would be 00201 = 201, which is NOT
// >= 10000 and would read as a production build. TUNO therefore anchors the
// beta series explicitly:  beta build = 10000 + cycle*100 + iteration.
// Cycle 2, iteration 1 → 10201 → renders v1.0.2-beta.1. isBeta stays the
// same >= 10000 test, and the two series stay disjoint at any cycle number.
// ======================================================================
const APP_BUILD = {
  version: "1.0",
  cycle: 3,          // hand-set cycle name — first beta cycle after production 2
  build: 10543,      // beta series: 10000 + cycle*100 + iteration -> v1.0.3-beta.243
  date: "2026-09-01",
  // When this build was cut, UTC — set with `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%MZ`,
  // never by hand (a local time typed into a UTC field puts the sign-in
  // stamp an hour into the future; ENCA builds 25090-25092 proved it).
  released: "2026-09-01T21:20Z",
  get isBeta() { return this.build >= 10000; },
  get stamp() { return `${this.label} · ${this.releasedLocal}`; },
  get releasedLocal() {
    const raw = String(this.released || "");
    const d = new Date(raw);
    if (isNaN(d.getTime())) return raw.replace("T", " ");
    try {
      const parts = new Intl.DateTimeFormat(undefined, {
        year: "numeric", month: "2-digit", day: "2-digit",
        hour: "2-digit", minute: "2-digit", hourCycle: "h23", timeZoneName: "short",
      }).formatToParts(d);
      const g = (t) => (parts.find((x) => x.type === t) || {}).value || "";
      if (!g("year")) return raw.replace("T", " ");
      const zone = g("timeZoneName");
      return `${g("year")}-${g("month")}-${g("day")} ${g("hour")}:${g("minute")}${zone ? ` ${zone}` : ""}`;
    } catch { return raw.replace("T", " "); }
  },
  get releasedUtc() { return String(this.released || "").replace("T", " ").replace("Z", " UTC"); },
  get timeZone() { try { return Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().timeZone || ""; } catch { return ""; } },
  // beta build = 10000 + cycle*100 + iteration (see the header) — so the
  // iteration is what remains above the anchored cycle base, unbounded past 99.
  get iteration() {
    return this.isBeta ? this.build - 10000 - this.cycle * 100 : this.build % 100;
  },
  get label() {
    return this.isBeta
      ? `v${this.version}.${this.cycle}-beta.${this.iteration}`
      : `v${this.version}.${this.build}`;
  },
  get full() { return `${this.label} · ${this.date}`; },
};

// Per-tool versions, keyed by the tile id in index.html. Same discipline as
// ENCA: each tool moves at its own pace; bump the tool you touched.
//
// `t` is THE TOOL'S PERMANENT NUMBER — assigned in the order the tool entered
// TUNO, NEVER reused, and a new tool takes the next free number in the same
// commit that adds the tile. ❓ Help, 📋 What's new and 🗺 Roadmap are
// deliberately not numbered: they are the app describing itself.
//
// A TOOL IN PRODUCTION STARTS AT 1.0.x (Mihai's rule, build 10505). The
// version number says which CHANNEL a tool has reached, not how finished it
// feels: everything on beta only is 0.x, and the day a tool is promoted it
// takes 1.0.0 and counts up from there. A 1.x on this channel is therefore
// a bug in the bookkeeping, not a compliment — T20 carried one from 10486
// to 10504 and was corrected back to 0.13 when the rule was written down.
//
// The counter CARRIES ACROSS the promotion rather than resetting, so every
// note below still describes the version number it is filed under: T01 went
// 0.27 -> 1.0.27, not back to 1.0.0. A tool promoted with no beta history
// would start at 1.0.0.
//
// WHICH TOOLS ARE IN PRODUCTION IS NOT REMEMBERED, IT IS READ — the tiles
// that exist in `git show main:index.html`, which is the same rule
// js/promote.js already applies to its queue. Eighteen were renumbered at
// 10505 from that list; T19, T20 and T21 are beta-only and stayed 0.x.
// CHIPS ARE CHANNEL
// LANGUAGE (production build 10 / beta 10450): the BETA chip lives on THIS
// channel only — here it says "still proving itself". On production a tool
// wears NEW (new to the current production build) or UPDATED (changed in
// it), or nothing; BETA there would name the channel the visitor is not on.
const TOOL_VERSIONS = {
  toolCompEv: { t: 26, v: "0.2", note: "v0.2 (build 10538, the layout round \u2014 Mihai picked Option A off the mockup): THE RAIL \u2014 the three framework control tables stop stacking under the capability list; a sticky left rail (the shared ep-rail chrome, T20/T19's own) holds Capabilities plus one node per framework, each carrying evidence-over-total with findings in red, and the pane swaps on click. One pane at a time, the framework pane repeats the disclaimer (it travels with what an auditor screenshots), state survives re-renders, a fresh read returns to Capabilities. BETA \u2014 R37, after the compliance-evidence feature of Ugur Koc's IntuneDocumentation (Elastic 2.0 \u2014 the FEATURE reimplemented from what the app demonstrably does, checked against its published coverage, sharing none of its code or tables; T05's arrangement): the technical evidence an auditor asks for, read from the tenant's own policies. TWO HALVES: 21 platform CAPABILITIES (Windows/macOS/iOS-iPadOS/Android \u2014 disk encryption, host firewall, real-time antimalware, ASR-any-rule-blocking, device lock, automatic updates, Secure Boot/SIP/Gatekeeper/jailbreak-and-integrity attestation, minimum OS) each detected by SETTING IDENTITY \u2014 settings-catalog definition ids (choice tails peeled, group children walked) and typed properties on device configurations and compliance policies \u2014 every hit an EVIDENCE ROW naming policy, setting, observed value and reach; then three FRAMEWORKS \u2014 ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A clause-8 selections, NIST SP 800-53 r5, NIST CSF 2.0 (Mihai's pick; BSI and SOC 2 can follow as queue items) \u2014 where control numbers are the frameworks' own and every summary sentence is WRITTEN HERE, because the standards' texts are licensed and an original description is the only honest thing to ship. THE HONESTY RULES ARE THE TOOL: evidence in a policy reaching nobody is NOT enforcement (reach by construction via EndpointSec.reachOf \u2014 one step past the original's assigned-or-not); a compliance policy MARKS rather than enforces, said on the row (the block lives in Conditional Access, which ENCA reads and TUNO does not); absence of evidence is absence of EVIDENCE, never proof of absence; a platform with no policies at all is SET ASIDE as not-managed-here rather than dragging controls to partial on a Windows-only tenant; NO SINGLE SCORE, and the disclaimer rides every export. The read is the shared policy cache (warm start, source line, T19's rule) or a fresh collect at the click \u2014 reads only, no new scope. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolDeviceCleanup: { t: 25, v: "1.0.2", note: "v0.2 (build 10533): THE SELECTION CONTROLS SURFACE — Select all / Select none moved ABOVE each table (they sat below a 21-row list, which is where controls go to be undiscovered — Mihai asked for what already existed, the surest sign of a placement bug), with a live 'n of N ticked' count beside them; the header master checkbox stays. — v0.1 (build 10532): BETA — R36, the Entra stale-device cleanup. TWO DELIBERATE STEPS, Mihai's spec: 30 days of directory silence → DISABLE (reversible); disabled AND 90 days silent → DELETE — a 90-day device still enabled is a disable candidate, never a delete, because the gap between the steps is where somebody notices the laptop in the drawer still matters. THE READ is /devices with approximateLastSignInDateTime (Device.Read.All, held since 10330) — the name says APPROXIMATE and so does every surface; a device with NO last sign-in at all is UNKNOWN, NOT STALE: its own actionless bucket, because an Autopilot object that never booted would otherwise read stale forever and be disabled on a guess. THE WRITES are TUNO's first directory-device writes — Device.ReadWrite.All, NEW at 10532, taken in the open per R18 (registration script + SECURITY.md in the same commit) — and Graph gates them on the signed-in user's DIRECTORY ROLE (Cloud Device Administrator / Intune Administrator among the allowed): the T18 rule, the second gate named before the run, a post-consent 403 translated as who-you-are. T11's pipeline sized for devices: every write preceded by a FRESH per-device read (signed in since the plan → refused, it woke up; re-enabled since → refused, somebody wants it back — this tool does not overrule people) and followed by a read-back (delete verified by the directory no longer returning it). Deletes arm only after typing DELETE, because the BitLocker recovery keys die with the object — said on screen and in the report; Autopilot-registered devices are refused by the service in its own words. Thresholds configurable (30/90 defaults). The 10531 selection pattern on both tables. 📝 ONE MARKDOWN REPORT — estate summary, every action with its outcome, everything deliberately NOT done and why, the unknowns — from the same state the screen renders. Managed (Intune-enrolled) devices wear a chip: deleting the directory object does not unenrol them" },
  toolMacBaseline: { t: 24, v: "1.0.7", note: "v0.7 (build 10531): SELECT ALL AND SELECT NONE on the upstream curation table — three faces of one selection: a master checkbox in the tick column's header (indeterminate when mixed, the familiar table pattern), the two explicit buttons beside the dry run, and the row ticks, all kept in step so none can lie about the others. The layout pass found cg-table already top-aligns its cells; the selection affordance was the real gap. — v0.6 (build 10530): THE BASELINE IS ALWAYS SHOWN — Mihai's rule: the catalog is bundled knowledge, so the Compare tab renders all 82 rows the moment the screen opens, before any read; 🍎 Read the tenant fills the right-hand columns. Until it does, the tenant side says NOT READ — never Missing, because unknown is not a verdict in this house, and a table of 82 'missing' claims before the first read would be 82 lies. A cold open (no cache, no consent) shows the same table; the read only ever adds. — v0.5 (build 10529): THE UPSTREAM PANEL SPEAKS THE TOOL'S OWN LANGUAGE, AND THE DIFF LEARNS VALUES. Layout (Mihai's 'fix this'): the summary is the same au-cards the Compare tab uses — new to us / matched-differs / covered / seen-not-comparable — not a chip strip this tool used nowhere else; the how-to collapses to one line; the table keeps its fixed layout. THE DIFF IS VALUE-AWARE: identity matching stays on setting-id sets, but covered now means covered — the same id carrying a DIFFERENT VALUE is a change (ours → theirs, both shown), because a changelog that missed it would bless drift; template references are normalised out, two tenants legitimately differ there. THE UPSTREAM CHANGELOG: every differs row folds open to its what's-new (added · changed · only-ours, values written out), and 📝 What's new (Markdown) writes the whole comparison down per policy — NEW sections, CHANGED sections with ours→theirs lines, the covered list — for the release notes the re-export deserves. — v0.4 (build 10528): THE FOUR ACTS ARE FOUR TABS — Mihai's layout, given in the request (which is the mockup round answered before it was asked): a seg at the top switches 🍎 Compare · 🧬 Export · 📥 Import · 🍏 Upstream, one act on screen at a time instead of one long scroll. Export and Upstream render only on the baseline tenant — off it the seg offers Compare and Import alone, the 10521 rule (an act that would be refused is not offered). The catalog line rides every tab; 🍎 Read the tenant stays global above the seg, since every act is cut from the read. The upstream curation table keeps its OWN host and is hidden, never destroyed, on other tabs — ticks and edited names survive the switch, the restore picker's rule applied to tabs. — v0.3 (build 10527): THE UPSTREAM WATCH, and the release tag learns what it always meant. Ryy.m IS A DATE — 26 the year, the x the month (Mihai's rule): R26.6 is June 2026, next January is R27.1, so releases compare YEAR-FIRST, never as decimals (26.12 after 26.6), and the bundled catalog's bare-month shorthand normalises through one function. THE WATCH (cfdev only, beside export — it authors): microsoft/intune-my-macs is never fetched — the CSP allows Graph and nothing else, and that rule does not bend for a read-only repo; the zip is downloaded BY THE BROWSER (a link is navigation) and loaded like a restore archive. Its policies are Graph-shaped (settings-catalog exports with a UTF-8 BOM — found by parsing, stripped by rule — and compliance policies), so MATCHING IS BY CONTENT, never name: a settings-catalog policy IS its set of settingDefinitionIds, a compliance policy the properties it configures; identical sets are SAME, half-or-better overlap is a MATCH WITH ITS DIFF (their ids we lack, ours they lack), the rest is NEW. New and differing controls get an editable canonical name — the proposal stamps THIS MONTH's release and increases the version (new → v1.0; differs → the matched identity kept, release re-stamped, minor bumped), and looksBaseline() gates the dry run: a name that does not wear the convention would be invisible to the very comparison above. Creates ride Restore.plan/apply in the dev tenant, unassigned; then re-export, and the export IS the new baseline. The curation table renders into its own host — ticks and edited names are DOM state, the restore picker's rule. — v0.2 (build 10526): THE REFERENCE EXPORT CORRECTED THE IDENTITY MODEL, the same day. v0.1 matched on 'R26.xx' as an ENCA-style per-policy number; the real cloudfellows.dev export (83 policies) collapsed onto two numbers, because R26.x is the BASELINE RELEASE worn by every policy — ENCA's own release line. The identity is now the NAME with the release tag and version stripped and separators normalised ('DCP-' and 'DCP -' are the reference tenant's own spelling drift, keyed identically); releases compare BEFORE versions (an R26.4 sighting is outdated next to the R26.6 catalog whatever its version says), versions break the tie; the number-clash bucket left with the number identity — a name identity cannot produce one. THE CATALOG IS BUNDLED: js/macbaselineData.js, the reference export verbatim minus one recorded departure — the tenant carried 'MACOS - CMP - Device Security - U - Security Requirments - R26.6 - v3.0' TWICE (identical release+version, a leftover copy); the catalog holds it once, and the tool shows the tenant's copy as ×2. 82 policies: 41 settings catalog, 7 device configurations, 3 compliance, 4 assignment filters, 27 shell scripts. IMPORT GAINS THE FILTER ROAD: assignment filters create through T14's OWN Filters.create (POST + read-back), collision-checked against a fresh Filters.list; scripts are identified but NOT importable — the shared read returns no script bodies, and a script without its body cannot be put back (restore's rule, on the row). — v0.1 (build 10525): BETA — R35, ENCA's Baseline Policies machinery Intune-side-out, pointed at macOS. THE NAME IS THE IDENTITY, exactly as ENCA's CA numbers: a baseline policy starts with MACOS, carries its permanent R26.xx release number, and ends with a version — matched by number, versions compared segment-wise (ENCA's cmpVersion verbatim, 1.0.10 > 1.0.9), and a number match the rest of the name contradicts is a NUMBER CLASH, never a silent match (ENCA's token-similarity corroboration, minus the CA personas macOS does not have). The buckets are ENCA's verbatim: ok / outdated / ahead / present / unversioned / missing / conflict / extra. THE CATALOG IS THE CLOUDFELLOWS.DEV REFERENCE EXPORT: on that tenant, and only there (the cfdev convention — TunoTenant.isCfdev, said on screen where it lives, never a hidden mode), the tool offers EXPORT, writing names, numbers, versions AND raw policy bodies (settings-catalog settings and ADMX definition values pulled from the detail read) into one file that drives identification and import everywhere; bundled as js/macbaselineData.js (BASELINE_MACOS) when regenerated, or loaded per session — never fetched at runtime, the CSP only allows Graph. IMPORT rides T04-restore's OWN pipeline (existingNames, plan, apply — collision stop, fresh per-create name check, read-back verify, ADMX child writes with rollback) with ONE departure: canonical baseline names, no [Restored] prefix, because a prefixed baseline would read as missing forever; a name collision is skipped, which is what 'present' means one screen up. Everything arrives UNASSIGNED — reach is T11's act. Reads ride the shared cache (warm start, source line); the import's write scope is the config write the registration already holds. Reads + create-only writes, no new scope" },
  toolRestrictedAu: { t: 23, v: "1.0.2", note: "v1.0.2 (build 10543, the layout round): THE CHIPS PIN (T19's fix) \u2014 the filter chips and the search move into a sticky .toolbar above the unit cards; the no-scoped-admin warning stays in the card it describes. BETA — R34, ported from ENCA's T27 and DELIBERATELY NARROWER. Create and manage RESTRICTED MANAGEMENT ADMINISTRATIVE UNITS: list every unit with its members and its scoped administrators, create one, rename it, delete it, add and remove members, grant and revoke scoped role grants. ENCA's version is wound through its Conditional Access baseline — persona vaults named from CA numbers, a baseline checklist, a group-to-persona map, prefix scans over CAB-SEC naming — and NONE of that came across, because TUNO has no CA baseline and inventing one here would import a vocabulary the tool cannot honour. Three Entra rules the screen is built around: the isMemberManagementRestricted FLAG IS IMMUTABLE, so the editor changes name and description only and says so; A UNIT WITH NO SCOPED ADMINISTRATOR IS A VAULT NOBODY CAN OPEN, so creating a restricted one without naming a keyholder is REFUSED and the field is prefilled with the signed-in account — whoever creates the unit is its administrator by default; and A ROLE-ASSIGNABLE GROUP INSIDE A RESTRICTED UNIT IS 🧊 FROZEN, its members editable by nobody at all, flagged with T22 named as the way out. Members are typed by the OData CAST rather than by @odata.type (T22's 10508 lesson), which is also the only way to read isAssignableToRole. Deleting a unit does not delete its members and the confirm is TYPED, because the shield comes off every member at once. And ENCA's settle() came across intact: Entra writes are not read-your-writes, so an optimistic change is re-applied across a 500/1200/2500ms backoff and reports honestly when the directory never catches up." },
  toolGroupMigrate: { t: 22, v: "1.0.8", note: "v1.0.8 (build 10543, the layout round): THE CHIPS PIN (T19's fix) \u2014 the filter chips and the local search move into a sticky .toolbar above the group table. v0.7 (build 10515): THE EXAMINE POPOUT NO LONGER TAKES THE TAB BAR WITH IT. It is a working pane you leave open while you think, not a dialogue that has to be answered — and a full-screen backdrop over the header and the tab bar meant closing it to reach another tool, which is the opposite of what putting it in a modal was for. A .keep-nav variant starts below the sticky nav and sits under both bars in the stacking order, so the tabs, the ＋ menu and the home button stay live while it is up; it still covers the content it acts on, and still closes by button, backdrop or Escape. Deliberately NOT applied to the edit and delete dialogues in T23: those ask a question that must be answered before anything else happens, and they should block. — v0.6 (build 10511): EXAMINE OPENS OVER THE LIST, NOT INSTEAD OF IT. Deciding a migration means comparing one group against the rest, and a screen that swapped the list out for the detail made that a matter of memory — the detail is a gu-modal now, the same one T02 and T07 use, and the list stays put underneath. Nine surfaces take a while, so the modal opens FIRST and holds its own progress. Backdrop and Escape close it, and the delegated handlers are bound to BOTH hosts because the modal lives outside the body they were on. THE CHIPS ARE FILTERS, T19's pattern: all / carries a membership rule / no destination worked out, clicking the one in force clears it, and a filter that would empty the list is hidden unless it is the one in force. Only chips that CAN filter became buttons — the restricted-unit count and the detected prefix are facts about the tenant rather than properties of a group, and dressing them as filters would promise a narrowing that cannot happen. AND WHOEVER CREATES THE UNIT IS ITS ADMINISTRATOR BY DEFAULT: the scoped-administrator field is prefilled with the signed-in account, because a restricted unit blocks every tenant-wide role and the person standing one up without naming a keyholder has locked themselves out of it. Prefilled, not forced. — v0.5 (build 10510): THE PERMISSIONS CARD BECOMES A BUTTON. 10509 put the whole permission inventory on the screen — three tables above the tool it was serving, taller than the tool itself — and an inventory is something you consult once, not something that sits over the work. It is now one 🔐 Grant permissions button in the same action row as Read, with the full list on its tooltip: every scope, what each one buys, and the warning that three of them write to the DIRECTORY rather than to Intune. The consent screen Microsoft shows carries the list again at the moment it matters, so nothing is hidden by the shrink. The per-scope ✓/○ went with the card rather than being squeezed into the tooltip: it reported what THIS SESSION had acquired, which is not what the tenant has consented, and two states that look alike and mean different things are worse in a tooltip than in a table — the button says what it will DO instead. — v0.4 (build 10509): FOUR THINGS THE SCREEN WAS MISSING. 🔐 A PERMISSIONS CARD that grants everything the tool will ever ask for in ONE prompt instead of four spread across a run — every scope listed with what it buys, the three directory writes marked as such, and the incremental route left exactly as it was: pressing Read without the button still asks only for the read scopes, so a read-only visit still ends holding no write scope. The ✓ says THIS SESSION HOLDS IT, not \"the tenant consented\", because a page can see its own tokens and not the tenant's grants. 🔍 A SEARCH BOX over the group list — local, over the list already in hand, matching name or object id; a tenant-backed typeahead here would re-read groups the screen has read and suggest ones this tool cannot act on. ✍️ TENANT AUTOFILL on the scoped administrator, through the app's one Suggest component rather than a second typeahead; it fills the UPN, and because Suggest writes straight into the value and dispatches nothing, the fields are SYNCED before use rather than subscribed to. 🧹 ARCHIVED CLEANUP: tick the leftovers of earlier migrations and delete them — but the reference check runs FIRST and a group anything still points at is REFUSED rather than warned about, changing the selection invalidates the check, and delete is locked until it has run, because unknown is not the same as unused. One batched read answers for the whole selection instead of thirteen surfaces per group. — v0.3 (build 10508): THE ADMINISTRATIVE-UNIT READ HAD THE WRONG SCOPE, and the screen blamed the wrong call. 10507 handed Graph.SCOPES.directory to /directory/administrativeUnits — a constant named `directory` that is [User.Read.All, Group.Read.All] and NOT Directory.Read.All — so the tenant answered \"Insufficient privileges to complete the operation\", and because both reads sat in one try/catch the screen printed \"Could not read the groups\" about the call that had already succeeded. Administrative units now carry their own scope (AdministrativeUnit.Read.All, the least privileged for that collection, listed beside ReadWrite.All because Entra consents by NAME); the two reads report separately, each naming the permission it wanted; and a failed unit read no longer takes the group list down with it — it becomes a warning card saying nothing can be migrated until it works, because without it the tool cannot see the 🧊 frozen case. MEMBER TYPING NOW ASKS THE DIRECTORY INSTEAD OF INFERRING: the old split read @odata.type off a $select response, which Microsoft's own documented example returns WITHOUT it, and treated absent as \"user\" — so a service principal, which Entra permits in a role-assignable group, would have been counted as a user and silently not copied. The cast answers which members are users and the uncast id list gives the total; any difference is refused, naming the object id. Five delegated scopes added to New-TunoAppRegistration.ps1 in the open, three of them directory writes. — v0.2 (build 10507): ADMINISTRATIVE UNITS LIVE UNDER /directory ON v1.0. ENCA talks to /beta, where an administrative unit is a top-level /administrativeUnits; TUNO talks to v1.0, where it is not — and 10506 answered every read with \"Resource not found for the segment 'administrativeUnits'\" on the first real tenant it met, before the tool could list a single group. Everything about the resource is GA on v1.0, isMemberManagementRestricted included, so the fix is the right path rather than a jump to beta for a directory write: one AU constant for the list, the create, the member $ref and the scoped-role grant, plus v1.0's documented $/ cast form on the memberOf read. The headless fake now REFUSES the flat path the way the tenant did, so this cannot come back quietly. — BETA, R33, ported from ENCA's ⑦ Migrate. Turns a ROLE-ASSIGNABLE security group into a plain one inside a RESTRICTED MANAGEMENT ADMINISTRATIVE UNIT. Role-assignable was only ever a way to keep membership away from tenant-wide group administrators; a restricted unit does that and lets you NAME who may manage it, without the 500-per-tenant cap, the ban on dynamic membership or the loss of nesting control. The two can never be combined — a role-assignable group admits only GA/PRA and a restricted unit blocks exactly those two — so a group that is both is 🧊 FROZEN, its members editable by nobody; this tool refuses to create that state and names it where it exists. isAssignableToRole is IMMUTABLE, so this is a RECREATE and the new group has a NEW OBJECT ID: T11's engine repoints the four surfaces the registration already permits (drift check and verify read-back included, not a second copy of them), everything else Intune points at the group is READ AND NAMED rather than moved, and anything outside Intune is declared invisible. The original is renamed aside as the rollback, never deleted. Refuses on: a held directory role, a non-user member, an incomplete assignment read, no destination unit, and a new unit with no scoped administrator — a restricted unit nobody is scoped to is a vault nobody can open. Units follow the tenant's INT- convention, suggested per group and overridable before anything is applied." },
  toolSecureScore: { t: 21, v: "1.0.5", note: "v0.5 (build 10504): ONE READ PER SESSION, NOT ONE PER SCREEN. The reading is cached with the moment it was fetched and both screens share it, so T20's Secure Score node adopts whatever T21 has already read instead of asking the tenant \u2014 and asking for a SecurityEvents consent prompt \u2014 a second time for an answer that is in memory. 10500 had refused to reuse it, on the argument that T20 could not state such a reading's age; that was the wrong conclusion from a real constraint, because the fix for an unstateable age is to state it. The node now says how long ago it was fetched and at what time, that it is shared with this tool, and offers \u21bb Read it again for a fresh one. T21's own Read button still always goes to the tenant \u2014 an explicit click means a fresh read. v0.4 (build 10503): THE CATEGORY BARS ARE FILTERS \u2014 T19's rule, its surface stat cards being its filters, and the argument is the same here: a category bar answers \"how is Apps doing\" and the only next question anybody has is \"which Apps controls\". Click a bar and the screen goes to that category's improvement actions with ITS OWN SCORE above them \u2014 points, gaps, and Microsoft's two comparison figures for that category rather than the tenant's. A chip row on the actions tab carries the same filter so it is visible and clearable without going back for it, and the list's Category cell narrows too (checked before the row's own open handler, so clicking the category filters and clicking anywhere else on the row opens). One piece of state behind all three, so they cannot disagree about what the screen is showing. Clicking an active filter clears it \u2014 a filter you cannot undo where you set it is a trap \u2014 and a filter carried into a view that empties it says which category emptied it and offers the way out, because by then its own chip is gone. Chips only exist for categories actually present in the current view: \"Data (0)\" under the achieved list is a control that leads nowhere. THE EXPORTS STAY WHOLE-TENANT and say so on the filtered card: a file called Secure Score holding only Apps is a trap, so the filter narrows the screen and not the download. v0.3 (build 10502, the first live-tenant round): THE CATALOGUE TEXT IS HTML AND NOTHING SAYS SO. Graph returns description, remediation and remediationImpact containing br, strong, p, ol/li, a href, &lsquo; and &rsquo; — often malformed. 10500 escaped it and printed it, so a card read \"&lsquo;<strong>Enable mailbox intelligence&rsquo;</strong>\". Rendering it as HTML was never an option: that hands a third party's markup an injection point in a page holding a tenant token. So SecureScore.plain() READS the markup and throws it away — structure becomes newlines and bullets, a link keeps its label AND its href because the URL is frequently the actual instruction, entities decode — and the plain text is escaped on render like any other string. It runs in controlsFrom, so the screen, the Markdown, the CSV and T20's correlation all read one clean shape. THE LAYOUT OVERFLOWED because a grid item's default min-width is min-content and Microsoft's remediation carries eighty-character portal URLs with nothing to break on: one card pushed its track past 340px, the row past the container, and the whole PAGE grew a horizontal scrollbar with the left column sliding under the sidebar. min-width:0 and overflow-wrap:anywhere, both needed. 🗂 CARDS | ☰ LIST — the seg T19, T20 and T14 wear, cards leading because the assessment text is the substance of a control and a table column cannot hold it; the list is for the tenant with fifty-six of these. Both faces open ONE popout carrying the full unclipped text, so the list is denser rather than lossier, and the card clamps to five lines with \"open for the full text\". And Microsoft's real impact vocabulary: the reference documents low/moderate/high, live tenants answer Low/Medium/High and very often the literal \"Unknown\" — all of them are mapped, Unknown weighs as MODERATE rather than falling through an unwritten default, and the card prints Microsoft's own word with \"weighed as moderate\" beside it. v0.2 (build 10501): THE SCREEN WAS BLANK AFTER A SUCCESSFUL READ. T21 shipped owning the ids ssBody, ssMd and ssProg — which T10 settings search already owned, and T10's screen comes FIRST in the document. getElementById answers with the first match, so every render wrote into the settings-search screen while T21's own stayed empty: no error, no empty-state message, nothing, after a consent prompt the admin had just answered. The ids are now scBody/scMd/scProg and the whole tool's prefix moves ss- to sc-, markup and stylesheet together. THE HEADLESS SUITE HAD NOT CAUGHT IT BECAUSE IT ASKED THE SAME WRONG QUESTION: the render tests called getElementById(\"ssBody\") exactly as the tool did, both got T10's div, and the assertions passed on content sitting in another tool's screen. Two checks close it — a document-wide duplicate-id assertion (it catches all three when run against 10500's index.html), and a render test that reaches through T21's own <section> rather than trusting an id lookup. A test that shares the bug's assumption is not a test. v0.1 (build 10500) — BETA — R02, the tenant's Microsoft Secure Score made explorable: the gauge and the two comparison bars, the per-category breakdown with Microsoft's global and similar-tenant figures as TICKS ON EACH BAR rather than numbers in a footnote, the timeline of every reading Graph still holds with improved and regressed across it, and the improvement actions ranked CHEAPEST POINTS FIRST. Shaped after GCIT's Export-SecureScoreReports (Elliot Munro) — that report's anatomy, rebuilt in a browser tab against one signed-in tenant instead of a partner-side sweep across GDAP customers. THREE THINGS SAID RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The cheapest-points order is TUNO'S, NOT MICROSOFT'S, and says so wherever it renders: points available weighted down by the userImpact and implementationCost Microsoft publishes, a control declaring neither treated as MODERATE — calling an unstated impact low is how a quick win becomes Monday's incident, and Microsoft's own stack ranking sits beside it as its own sort. The comparison figures come back with no stated unit, so a value above 100 is rendered as POINTS rather than as an impossible percentage. And a control with no readable score or ceiling is counted as NEITHER achieved nor a gap, listed by name. GRAPH KEEPS ABOUT NINETY DAYS, which is why the tool exports a versioned JSON snapshot carrying the readings AND the catalogue they were read under (a title and a max score change under you, and an old point re-labelled with today's profile is a quiet lie) and uploads them back: the timeline then reaches as far as somebody kept files, uploaded points drawn as their own marks, a same-day clash resolving to the LIVE reading with the count it dropped stated, and a snapshot from a different tenant REFUSED BY TENANT ID — two tenants in one timeline is not a comparison, it is a wrong graph. This is also the house's ONE READER of security/secureScores: T20's Secure Score node calls SecureScore.collect() rather than reading it a second time, the T05 rule on a second surface. SecurityEvents.Read.All on the click, reads only." },
  toolPosture: { t: 20, v: "1.0.14", note: "v0.14 (build 10517): A PARTLY-ENFORCED STATEMENT NOW SAYS WHAT HAPPENS TO THE REST OF THE FLEET. rolloutLine() returned null whenever nothing was staged, so a statement headed \u0022partly enforced on 0.4% of the fleet · 9909 not yet targeted\u0022 went silent about those 9909 — a rollout brief that reports a hole and not its plan is worse than one that reports neither. Every partial statement now carries a 🎯 line in place, under Already enforced today (Mihai's pick, Option A): \u0022NO CHANGE — nothing is staged for the remaining 9852 of 9947\u0022 where the remainder is countable, and where an assignment FILTER is what narrows it, the line says the filter is the cause, not the assignment, and refuses to quote a remainder a browser cannot compute. partlyEnforced() becomes the ONE predicate behind both the heading word and the new line, so the two can never disagree; verdictWord() now reads the whole reach object rather than a bare number, which also stops a filter-capped sum at or above the fleet from being headed \u0022enforced now\u0022. And where a live partial has a staged policy that is ITSELF partial, the destination number answered the wrong question — coverage AFTER rollout is the union of two sets whose membership nobody read, so it is stated as a range (at least the larger, at most the sum) and refused outright if either side is a filter ceiling or an unread count. One implementation: the Markdown, the Word export and the pane all read the same rolloutLine(). v0.13 (build 10505): \u2630 LIST IS THE DEFAULT FACE, reversing 10477 on the live tenant's evidence \u2014 Account protection came back with 33 policies, Attack surface reduction with 23, Edge-in-catalog with 23, and a wall of thirty-three cards is a scroll where thirty-three rows are a screen. The card face is one click away and unchanged. ALSO RENUMBERED, and downwards: 1.0 was cut at 10486 and 1.1 at 10488 while this tool was and still is beta-only. Under the rule written into this file's header at 10505, the version says which CHANNEL a tool has reached \u2014 0.x on beta, 1.0.0 the day it is promoted \u2014 so a 1.x here was bookkeeping wearing a compliment. The history is unchanged; only the number it is counted under is. v1.3 (build 10504): the \ud83d\udcca node ADOPTS the reading this session already has. If T21 has run \u2014 or T20 read it earlier \u2014 opening the node shows the matrix immediately, with the age of the numbers and where they came from stated on it, rather than offering a button for work already done and a second consent prompt for an answer in memory. A fresh posture read still clears the correlation, and opening the node re-adopts. v1.2 (build 10500): 📊 SECURE SCORE GAPS — a fourth rail node, and the one thing neither tool can say alone. These checks read POLICY: what is configured and whether it reaches anybody. Microsoft Secure Score reads THE ESTATE: what devices actually report. They disagree constantly, and the disagreement IS the finding — a check that passes while Microsoft still holds the points is almost never the policy, it is machines that never onboarded to Defender for Endpoint, a licence the control needs, or devices that have not reported since the policy was written, and that bucket leads the screen because the work is already done and is not being counted. Four buckets: both agree open, configured-but-unscored, Microsoft-scores-it-and-no-check-covers-it (a gap in the CHECK SET, shown rather than hidden), and findings Microsoft does not score at all (App Control in audit mode blocks nothing whatever the score says). PAIRS ARE FALSIFIABLE BY CONSTRUCTION: a check is tied to a control by an explicit regex on Microsoft's own control id or published title, every pair PRINTS BOTH NAMES so a wrong match is visible rather than load-bearing, and a control matching nothing is listed under Microsoft's title — so a miss can only ever under-correlate, never mis-correlate. The read is T21's SecureScore.collect(), asked at THIS click under SecurityEvents.Read.All: a separate button, because the posture read itself still costs no new permission and the tile still says so. A fresh posture read clears the correlation rather than pairing new checks with an old score. The impact brief keeps its end-user contract — the gaps ride as APPENDIX B, behind a line saying to cut it before the mail is sent — in the Markdown, the Word export and the on-screen report alike. v1.1 (build 10488, the review round): five ways the new numbers could still have lied \u2014 a worker-caught count failure summing as a confident zero, an unsound \u0022at most\u0022 on a bound that is floored and capped at once, excluded-only briefed as unassigned, an RBAC read two other tools never consented to, and a floor caveat after a sentence saying there is no sum. v1.0 (build 10486): THE BRIEF SAYS BOTH NUMBERS \u2014 \ud83d\udcdf enforced now on N and \ud83c\udfaf at rollout: targets \u2026 of N, today and the destination side by side, the second READ off the statement\u0027s own staged policies rather than assumed to be the whole fleet. v0.9 (build 10484): A FILTERED ASSIGNMENT STATES A CEILING \u2014 All devices plus a filter had been reported as the whole fleet, which is the one arithmetic error that overstates protection; a filter only narrows, so reach says at most, the missing side says at least, and the filter that caps it is named. v0.8 (build 10483): THE DEVICE COUNTS LAND \u2014 Graph.pool\u0027s { item, value } wrapper was read as the count itself, so every group member count was null and every reach line claimed ~0 of the fleet with a (floor) footnote; a sum with nothing readable in it now says UNKNOWN, and a partial one counts up. v0.7 (build 10482): filtered assignments are named on the cards and the list rows, and the scope union gains the assignment-filter read that names them. v0.6 (build 10481, the first live-tenant round): AUDIT MODE IS READ FROM THE POLICY CONTENT, NEVER ASSUMED — the OIB WDAC policies said 'Enabled:Audit Mode' in their XML while the brief claimed blocking, the wrong-direction error this house exists to prevent. catalogRows stamps an audit flag from the RAW value (the display row loses the word to tail-shortening and the 300-char cap); App Control statements and the appctl check are three-way — enforce claims blocking, audit says inventorying-not-blocking, unreadable says unknown. Card and list reach cells carry the device numbers (~N of D devices · M still missing); at-rollout brief statements state the whole fleet; a retired (unassigned) interim policy is dropped from the brief entirely — not today, not the plan. v0.5 (build 10480): (TO-BE-REMOVED) IS A THIRD TEMPORAL STATE — Mihai's tenant convention: such policies are in place NOW and are phased out at rollout. The brief speaks it (⏳ interim chips, 'staged replacement takes over' vs a new WHAT STOPS AT ROLLOUT section when nothing is staged, the appendix wearing 'enforced now — interim, retired at rollout'), and the checks flag it in ONE place: a PASS whose every reaching policy is interim becomes PASS — INTERIM ONLY, counted as a finding, naming the staged replacement or the coming gap. And the 'enforced now' brief statements carry their 📟 device count — target group vs the whole fleet, the findings' arithmetic spoken shorter. v0.4 (build 10479): 📟 DEVICES ON EVERY FINDING — how many enrolled Windows devices a check's policies actually target and how many the tenant leaves out, assignment arithmetic worn honestly: tenant-wide is the fleet, groups summed by Graph.memberCount (the AppLocker deploy's seam), overlaps not deduplicated, exclusions not subtracted, filters cap at may, unreadable counts make the sum a floor — TARGETS, NOT CHECK-INS, every limit on the line. A GAP now says '0 of N — all N missing this control', T16's denominator idea carried into every check, on screen and in the export. Also the flush-card layout fix: the rail pane joins the .list-card padding convention (the 10395 disease in a new container shape). v0.3 (build 10478): 👁 READ THE FULL BRIEF — the finished communication renders on screen through TunoReport (ENCA's report viewer, ported this build as js/report.js — the .md-view styles had waited in the stylesheet since the scaffold), exactly what the Markdown export writes, same filename, with Copy and Download in the viewer: readable BEFORE anything is downloaded. v0.2 (build 10477): the pane toolbar is a CARD like everything else on the page — a bare input floating on the background read as unfinished on the first live screenshot — and it gains the 🗂 Cards | ☰ List seg: cards stay the default (Mihai's call, they read better), the list is the same policies as one .cg-table row each, row click opening the same popout. The choice sticks across nodes; a re-read resets it. v0.1 — BETA, R31, the portal's Endpoint security blade mirrored: discipline rail (plus MDE-in-catalog and Edge-in-catalog nodes found by setting definition id), ENCA T32's impact brief translated from sign-ins to devices with MD + Word export, and a best-practice analyser in ENCA's MSLearn check shape — severity, requirement, remediation, the learn.microsoft.com page per check, NOT REACHING its own verdict when the right setting sits in a policy reaching nobody. The read is the documenter's collect() (settings catalog section, now carrying templateFamily); disciplines split by T16's classifier; verdicts are the house claims. ShadowDeploy MDE cross-checked the defId families only — BUSL-licensed, so checked against, never ported." },
  toolOverview: { t: 19, v: "1.0.9", note: "v1.0.9 (build 10522): THE PLATFORM FILTER — T05's select, ported into the static toolbar (options rebuilt with counts over the whole collection; the control itself never re-rendered, the T15 rule). The platform words are Docs.platformsOf's — the one normaliser — so T19, T05 and T11 cannot disagree about the same policy; Not platform-specific means the policy declares none, and a platform the tenant has none of is not offered as an option. Joins surface and search ahead of the verdict chips, so the chips keep counting what you are looking at. — v1.0.8 (build 10521): THE POPOUT ACTS — ENCA's pcard-actions row ported Intune-side-out into the popout foot, narrowed to the acts TUNO has: ✏️ hands over to the Assignment editor with the policy SELECTED (offered only for the four surfaces T11 edits — an act that would be refused is not offered), 📄 downloads the documenter's own single-policy Markdown (the same Docs.markdown T05's popout copies), 🗄 downloads the policy as Graph returned it, deliberately titled a RECORD, not a restore file — restore stays T04's job and a button must not promise otherwise. What-if flow and Policy state were not ported: the first is T08's by group, the second a write a read-only screen has no business doing. — v1.0.7 (build 10520): THE WARM START — opening the tool shows the tenant when the shared policy cache holds it (the sign-in prefetch, or another tool's read), with the read time SAID in the notes line; a read still running at sign-in is joined, progress card and all, rather than raced. 🗂 Read the tenant becomes the refresh: always a fresh read, now THROUGH the cache so T11 and the next tool warm from this click. A cold cache is yesterday's tool exactly. — v0.6 (build 10514): THE SURFACES MOVE INTO A STICKY LEFT RAIL — T20's shape, Option A of a two-option mockup round. They shipped at 10458 as a horizontal grid of stat cards above the list, which read well and cost the top third of every screen; the rail gives them a permanent home and hands the full width to the answer. Each row carries its pair — assigned / configured — with a legend under the All surfaces row, so the counts survived the move rather than being traded for the space. The rail wears T20's OWN .ep-rail/.ep-node classes rather than a second rail that drifts away from it. An unreadable surface keeps BOTH homes, a red rail row and the note above the list, and is still not a filter: one red line in a rail is easier to skim past than the dashed card it replaced, and a 403 surface reading as empty is the most dangerous thing this tool could do. The cards/list face toggle, the verdict chips, the search and the shared popout are untouched. OPTION B IS PARKED RATHER THAN REJECTED — a chip strip of counts above the list with the rail carrying names only; if these numbers read too quietly, that strip is the change, and .ov-surf* stays in the stylesheet because it is what Option B would put back. — v0.5 (build 10505): group member counts on every assignment chip, for free \u2014 the documenter reads them now, so the policy overview shows them without asking the tenant anything it was not already asking. v0.4: v0.4 (build 10485): THE LIST FACE \u2014 \ud83d\uddc2 Cards | \u2630 List, T20\u0027s seg ported verbatim rather than a second spelling of the same control, cards still the default, the table carrying the card\u0027s own fields plus the surface a card wears as a chip, and a row click opening the same popout. v0.3 (build 10482): AN ASSIGNMENT FILTER SAYS ITS NAME \u2014 the reach cell wore \u2691 filter \u2014 may, which told a reader that something narrowed the assignment and refused to say what, while the portal shows the name beside the group; Docs names filters in the same pass it names groups and the chip carries name AND mode, because a name without its mode does not say which way the filter cut. v0.2 (build 10459): the read shows THE progress card — prog() delegates to TunoProgress like every other tool (10458 hand-rolled a text line, exactly the divergence the shared implementation exists to prevent), hosted in a plain #ovBody div rather than the .cards grid so the centred card is not a grid item. Original: BETA — R30, ENCA's list-policies view turned Intune-side-out: every configured object in the tenant as CLICKABLE CARDS, Option B of a two-option mockup round (surface cards as filters — the T09 pattern — over one flat grid; Option A's per-surface headings lost). THE READ IS T05's collect(), WHOLE — the T12 rule again, because a second copy of thirteen-surface reading is how two tools start disagreeing about one tenant — asked at the click as the same scope union T05's own run asks, directory read for group names included. A card click opens THE SAME POPOUT T05 opens: Docs.popoutHtml, extracted in this build so the template exists once (the redactValue lesson applied to markup), with each tool keeping its own foot — T05 ticks into the document, T19 just closes. THE VERDICT IS THE HOUSE CLAIM: Assigned = reaching somebody BY CONSTRUCTION (at least one include or tenant-wide target, T12/T26's rule); EXCLUDED-ONLY IS ITS OWN VERDICT, not a flavour of unassigned — the policy HAS assignments and every one says not-you, T09's distinction kept; an assignment filter caps reach at MAY, worn on the card. Per-device applicability is not evaluated and empty included groups are T09's finding, pointed at. The surface stat cards carry total · assigned · nobody per surface — 'what does this tenant even have' answered before any scrolling — and A SURFACE THAT COULD NOT BE READ IS A NAMED ⚠ CARD that is deliberately NOT a filter: filtering to a surface nobody read would be an empty grid pretending to be an answer. Search is live over names, types, descriptions, platforms, surface labels AND assignment group names ('which policies hit SG-Pilot'), in the static toolbar so typing survives re-renders (T15's lesson); chips count the surface+search set so they answer 'of what you are looking at'. The ENCA card classes (.scard, .state, .fchip) had lived unused in TUNO's stylesheet since the scaffold — this is the tool that wears them. Reads only; no write scope is reachable from the file. No exports on purpose: the document is T05's job, one click away in the same popout" },
  toolLaps: { t: 18, v: "1.0.2", note: "v1.0.2 (build 10542, the layout round): STICKY CHIPS (T19's fix) \u2014 the bucket filter gains a pinned compact face above the 200-fold device list: All plus the five buckets with counts, the same filter as the cards. The All chip clears. BETA — R29, after Ugur Koc's Get Windows LAPS Audit (MIT): which Windows devices have an escrowed local administrator password and how old it is — NOT ESCROWED and STALE are the two shapes of one finding, a LAPS policy that is not applying, found before the day somebody needs the password that is not there. THE READS: directory/deviceLocalCredentials (beta, metadata only — id, deviceName, lastBackupDateTime) cross-referenced against Intune Windows devices on the ENTRA DEVICE ID, the original's join key. PASSWORDS ARE NEVER RETRIEVED AND THERE IS NO SWITCH THAT WOULD: the scope is DeviceLocalCredential.ReadBasic.All — the FIRST NEW PERMISSION since Device.Read.All at 10330, taken in the open per the R18 rule, and ReadBasic deliberately because Graph cannot return a password value through it; the full Read.All stays with R10's helpdesk viewer if that ever ships. TWO GATES NAMED BEFORE THE RUN: the scope at the click, and Graph's own DIRECTORY-ROLE gate on the endpoint (Intune Administrator among the allowed) — a 403 after consent is detected and explained as 'who you are, not what TUNO may do', never a bare status code. FIVE BUCKETS, not the original's four: healthy / stale (threshold yours, default 60 days) / not escrowed / AGE UNKNOWN (escrowed, unparseable backup time — neither healthy nor stale, because either would be a guess) / UNMATCHABLE (no Entra device id on the enrolment record — the original counts these not-escrowed, which accuses a machine it never looked up). Escrow records no enrolled device claims (retired machines, passwords still held) are their own list, never silently ignored. Cards double as bucket filters (T09), rows fold on device ids (T03), click-through to the 🖥 Device analyzer. Reads only. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolMaa: { t: 17, v: "1.0.2", note: "v1.0.2 (build 10540, the layout round): THE RAIL \u2014 Overview | What is gated | Policies | Requests | Admins, one pane at a time in the shared ep-rail chrome; the nobody-can-open count, the ungated state and the pending requests are worn on the rail nodes in red. An empty Policies pane points at the Overview finding rather than standing blank. BETA — R27, after Ugur Koc's MAA Compliance Dashboard Report (MIT): whether multi-admin approval is protecting anything, which nothing in the portal shows. THE READS: operationApprovalPolicies and operationApprovalRequests (both beta, both under the RBAC read), the four gated inventories (apps, scripts, settings catalog, roles) as context, roleAssignments + transitive group members for the admins, approver groups transitive for who can approve — request status tolerated as string OR number because tenants answer both ways, the original's own hedge. COVERAGE IS COMPUTED THE WAY MAA WORKS: a policy gates its operation TYPE tenant-wide, so one app policy protects every app; a category with no policy has NO GATE, which is the finding; device-action types are listed as action gates, not inventories; an inventory that could not be read is UNKNOWN, never zero, and the gate verdict stands on the policy list. THE FINDING THE ORIGINAL CANNOT MAKE: a policy whose approver groups hold no members is A GATE NOBODY CAN OPEN — every operation it gates sits pending until someone is added; checked transitively, and a group that could not be read makes the count unknown, not zero. THE ADMINS TABLE CARRIES T07's SENTENCE verbatim on screen and in every export: Intune RBAC only — Global Administrator and Intune Administrator do not appear in deviceManagement/roleAssignments, so the accounts with the most access are not in the admins-without-MAA count. 'No MAA policies' is an answer, not an error, and reads as one. Approved requests without parseable timestamps sit OUTSIDE the approval-time numbers with their count stated. FEWER SCOPES THAN THE ORIGINAL: it asks six including Directory.Read.All; everything here rides already-consented reads, names via getByIds (R07's technique). Reads only, NO NEW SCOPE. Exports MD + requests CSV + admins CSV" },
  toolEndpointSec: { t: 16, v: "1.0.3", note: "v1.0.3 (build 10541, the layout round): THE DISCIPLINE CARDS BECOME THE RAIL \u2014 All + the six core disciplines + any extra the tenant actually has, sticky beside the list in the shared ep-rail chrome, verdict worn as the node's colour (red is a gap) and spelled out in its title. The unbounded whole-tenant policy list no longer buries the jump back to a discipline; same data-fwdisc contract, the All node clears. v1.0.2 (build 10535, Mihai's ask off the live screenshot): CONFIGURED ON HOW MANY DEVICES \u2014 every covering policy row wears the number its assignments claim, answered as GROUP MEMBERSHIP because that is the only count a browser can give without walking the fleet: include groups summed by Graph.memberCount (the AppLocker deploy's seam, T20's precedent), all-devices speaking the fleet denominator the tool already reads, all-users saying honestly that their devices follow them and no group holds that number. THE ARITHMETIC WEARS ITS LIMITS where the number is printed \u2014 overlapping groups are not deduplicated, exclusions are not subtracted (an exclusion group's members are never even asked for \u2014 counting them toward reach would add exactly the machines the assignment keeps out), a filter caps the claim at may, an unreadable count makes the sum a FLOOR marked 'at least', and a fleet or member count that could not be read is unknown, never zero. An empty include group prints 0 on purpose \u2014 that is T09's finding, worth seeing where the target is named. reachOf now tells all-devices and all-users apart (additive; the tenantWide flag is unchanged), the fold's group chips carry per-group counts, the Markdown gains a Configured on column, the CSV a configuredOn field, and one phrase-maker feeds screen and both exports so they cannot disagree about one number. The group read (Group.Read.All, already in the registration) joins the click's scope ask \u2014 at the click, never at sign-in. BETA — R26, after Ugur Koc's Get Firewall and ASR Status (MIT): whether the Windows fleet actually has firewall and attack-surface-reduction enforcement, OR JUST POLICY OBJECTS. Every endpoint security policy — settings catalog policies with an endpointSecurity template family, FILTERED CLIENT-SIDE because the server-side filter is unreliable on this surface (his note, kept) — plus the legacy security intents, grouped into the portal's own disciplines with a verdict each: covered / policies exist but none reaches anybody / no policy at all. 'COVERED' IS NARROWED FROM THE ORIGINAL: assigned AND reaching somebody BY CONSTRUCTION, the T12 rule — no include and no tenant-wide target is nobody, exclusions-only is nobody, an assignment filter caps reach at may; per-device applicability is not evaluated and the screen says so, and empty included groups are T09's finding, pointed at rather than half-repeated. LEGACY INTENTS ARE CLASSIFIED, NOT DUMPED — the original lists intents under a 'Legacy Intent' pseudo-discipline so a tenant enforcing firewall the old way reads as a firewall GAP, an overstatement in the dangerous direction; here the intent's template resolves it into its discipline (one read of deviceManagement/templates), an unclassifiable one counts toward nothing and says so, and the legacy surface's assigned-or-not-only answer is a caveat on the row. THE DEVICE COUNT IS THE DENOMINATOR: a firewall or ASR gap is that many machines on local defaults, and a count that could not be read is unknown, not zero. Group names via resolveNames (getByIds); unresolved names are stated, never silently GUIDs. Discipline cards double as filters, rows fold to their assignments (open set on policy ids, the T03 rule). Reads only, NO NEW SCOPE. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolDefender: { t: 15, v: "1.0.3", note: "v1.0.3 (build 10536, Mihai's ask, from his own Get-DefenderSettings.ps1): \ud83e\uddf1 THE MDE-ACTIVE BASELINE MATCH \u2014 the CloudFellows baseline ($baselineConfig from Configure-MDEActive.ps1 v2.0, 2025-10-28) bundled with provenance per the T24 rule, matched in TWO LAYERS because Graph splits the truth in two. THE POLICY LAYER reads the AV and ASR settings-catalog policies WITH their settings (config read joins the ask at that click, never sign-in) and matches 13 mapped settings plus all 19 ASR rules \u2014 per-rule catalog children AND the legacy guid=action string both understood \u2014 where a setting living only in a policy REACHING NOBODY reads reaches-nobody and never compliant (T20's rule; reach judged by EndpointSec.reachOf, the one reader), two reaching policies that disagree are a CONFLICT pointed at T12, a rule stricter than baseline still reads deviates (the match reports difference, not direction), and 11 baseline settings with no one-to-one catalog setting are listed NOT CHECKED rather than guessed. The script's own CloudBlockLevel ambiguity (stores 6 for HighPlus; its report calls 4 High+ and 6 zero tolerance) is carried as 4-or-6-satisfies with the ambiguity printed. THE DEVICE LAYER checks the three flags machines actually report \u2014 real-time, malware, NIS (networkInspectionSystemEnabled now read, additively; NOT a new finding, bucket semantics unchanged) \u2014 no-state devices excluded from checked and counted as unknown-not-compliant, not-reported flags never counted as deviation. NO SINGLE SCORE ON PURPOSE \u2014 the script gives a percentage; a conflict and a gap are not the same finding, so this screen counts verdicts. The match rides the MD export as its own section; the CSV gains networkInspection. A fresh fleet read clears the match rather than pairing new devices with old policy answers. v0.2 (build 10446) adds THE DEVICE SEARCH: a box in the static toolbar — typing survives the list re-rendering under it — that appears only AFTER a fleet has been read, because its suggestions come from the fleet and an autocomplete with nothing behind it is a promise nothing keeps. Suggestions are ranked (name-starts beats name-contains beats user/id), keyboard-navigable, and picking one filters the list to that device AND opens its fold — the point of finding a machine is reading it. Free typing is itself a live filter over name, user and exact id, combinable with the bucket cards; an empty query suggests NOTHING, because an autocomplete that lists the fleet unprompted is a dump, not a search. No Graph call per keystroke — the fleet is already in memory. Original: BETA — R25, after Ugur Koc's Get Defender Status Report (MIT): the machines where Defender is silently off or outdated, before they become incident tickets. TWO READS, BOTH BETA because that is where Graph keeps protection state: deviceProtectionOverview for the tenant rollup, then every Windows device's windowsProtectionState — real-time protection, tamper protection, malware protection, signature currency, overdue quick and full scans, pending reboot, a deviceState that is not clean — each device carrying its LIST of findings rather than one traffic light. THE PER-DEVICE READ IS THE WHOLE COST and the original pays it one device at a time with a sleep; folded into $batch here, twenty a trip, the R12/R19 technique. THREE HONESTY RULES: a device that never reported protection state is ITS OWN BUCKET, never healthy by omission — a machine Defender has never reported on is exactly the machine this report exists to find, and a state that could not be READ lands in the same bucket with its error stated (unknown is not clean, the T09 rule); DISABLED AND NOT-REPORTED ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS — the original counts a null flag as off, here a flag the device did not say reads 'not reported', per flag, on the row and in both exports; and the tenant rollup CAN DISAGREE with the per-device sums because Graph refreshes it on its own schedule — both shown, labelled, never reconciled. Cards double as bucket filters (the T09 pattern), rows fold open to the full state (open set keyed on device ids, the T03 rule), a device click lands in the 🖥 Device analyzer through the tile's own handler. Reads only, NO NEW SCOPE — both endpoints under the device read T06 brought in. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolFilters: { t: 14, v: "1.0.4", note: "v1.0.4 (build 10543, the layout round): THE BAR PINS (T19's fix) \u2014 .af-bar (view seg, search, platform select) goes sticky by one shared-CSS rule, so the controls never scroll away under the filter grid. v1.0.3 (build 10524): THE PLATFORM FILTER — a select in the bar beside the search, over the filter's OWN platform property (declared at creation, fixed for life — no derivation needed). Only platforms the tenant's filters use are offered, with counts over the whole list; composes with the search and the references chip. — v1.0.2 (build 10505): no change to this tool, and a large one to what it is FOR \u2014 FilterRules, built here for R32, is now what T20 uses to turn a filtered tenant-wide target from \"at most all 9964\" into a count. The evaluator was written to answer one screen's question and turned out to be the missing half of the whole house's reach arithmetic. v1.0.1: BETA — R21, after Alper Atar's IntuneShade (MIT): the filters themselves, at last. READ: every assignment filter with platform, rule and management type; per-filter USAGE as a separate optional read through GroupUse's OWN source table (the T06 rule — a surface added there is counted here), tenant-wide targets included because they carry filters too; the used-by column is ABSENT, NOT ZERO, until the scan runs, and an unread surface makes every zero a floor, said on screen. WRITE — the third thing in TUNO that writes, T11's rules sized for one object: write scope at the click; edit preceded by a FRESH READ with a drift stop keyed on lastModifiedDateTime (changed since load = REFUSED, decide against the current rule); every write verified by read-back field-for-field; delete confirmed by TYPING the name. THE GATE THE TOOL EXISTS FOR: delete refused while usage is non-zero, verified against the tenant's payload associations AT THE MOMENT OF THE ACT (one $expand=payloads read), never against the cached scan — and 'could not check' refuses too, because a filter deleted while in use silently widens every assignment that carried it to the whole target group. After a write the cached usage scan is dropped (absent again), not trusted stale. First PATCH and first DELETE in the application — added to the Graph layer under the existing no-retry write rule. Platform is fixed after creation (Graph's rule, surfaced in the form). Exports MD" },
  toolCompliance: { t: 13, v: "1.0.5", note: "v1.0.5 (build 10539, the layout round): THE RAIL \u2014 Estate | Coverage | Policies | Stale, one pane at a time in the shared ep-rail chrome, so the coverage table and every policy stop standing between you and the stale list. The rail nodes wear the findings \u2014 gaps, failing-policy count, stale count \u2014 in red; the platform filter stays where it was, inside the Policies pane it narrows; an empty Stale pane says WHY it is empty (nothing silent, or the estate unread \u2014 different answers); coverage-outranks-a-failing-setting now lives on the rail node rather than in card order. v1.0.4 (build 10524): THE PLATFORM FILTER on the policy list — platformOfPolicyType's own vocabulary, the one the coverage section already speaks, so the list and coverage cannot disagree. Narrows the LIST only: the estate cards, the coverage findings and the stale table keep counting the whole tenant, and the header says 'n of N' while narrowed. Offered only when the tenant has more than one platform. —BETA — R22 + R28. v0.3 (build 10428) adds the COVERAGE SECTION, after Ugur Koc's Get Compliance Policy Coverage (MIT): the platforms nothing evaluates — enrolled devices with no compliance policy that reaches anybody — computed ENTIRELY from the two reads the report already does (the R19 rule, zero new Graph traffic, no new scope). Platform identity is the script's own 1:1 OData-type mapping; device OSes normalise to T05's one vocabulary. COVERED IS THE R26 CLAIM: assigned AND reaching somebody by construction — exclusions-only and unassigned cover nothing, a filter caps the verdict at may (worn on the row), and group scoping WITHIN a platform is not evaluated, said on screen. A PLATFORM IS THREE ANSWERS: gap (devices, no covering policy), covered, and no-devices — context, not a gap, so an unassigned macOS policy in a Windows-only tenant does not read as a hole. THE ORIGINAL'S FOOTNOTE BECOMES A READ: deviceManagement/settings secureByDefault says whether unassigned devices are marked Compliant — the lean that decides whether a gap silently passes Conditional Access — stated next to the table, and an unreadable setting is said with the portal path, never guessed. Either half of the read failing makes coverage UNKNOWN, NOT UNCOVERED. Coverage CSV export joins the other three. — v0.2 (build 10415) applies the 10413 layout from the four-tool mockup round: the estate strip becomes FIVE STAT CARDS with percentages (compliant, non-compliant, in grace, stale with the also-count-compliant tension on the card itself, unknown-other), and the policy table becomes FOLDED ROWS that unfold in place to the full rollup, the failing settings worst-first, and the status gaps — the open set keyed on policy ids so a re-render keeps cards open, the T03 rule. Verdict accents: clean green, failing red, gap amber. Everything read is unchanged. Original: after Alper Atar's IntuneShade (MIT): the estate-wide compliance rollup TUNO answered only per-device (T06). One read: managedDevices for the estate (compliant / non-compliant / in grace / unknown, configManager named under other rather than dropped), deviceCompliancePolicies with assignments, then TWO CHEAP ROLLUPS PER POLICY folded into $batch — deviceStatusOverview and deviceSettingStateSummaries, with only the settings actually failing somewhere shown, worst first. STALE IS ITS OWN COLUMN, NOT A FLAVOUR OF COMPLIANT: threshold on screen (default 30 days, Intune's own status-validity default), a device that never synced counts stale, and a compliant-but-stale machine sits in BOTH columns with the tension stated — resolving it quietly in either direction would be a lie in that direction. The numbers are Graph's cheap rollup refreshed on Intune's schedule and labelled so; per-device drill-down is named as T06's job; a policy whose statuses could not be read is LISTED with a status-gap tag (unknown is not clean), an unassigned policy is a finding tag, and a failed estate or policy read marks that half unknown rather than zero. No new scope — devices came with T06, config with T04. Reads only. Exports MD, CSV, stale-devices CSV" },
  toolConflict: { t: 12, v: "1.0.5", note: "v1.0.5 (build 10542, the layout round): STICKY CHIPS (T19's fix) \u2014 verdict chips (All | can | may | cannot, with counts) and the platform select move into a sticky .toolbar, so re-filtering after a long conflict list never means scrolling back. Chips clear on re-click; a fresh scan resets them. v1.0.4 (build 10524): THE PLATFORM FILTER on the conflict list — a conflict wears the platforms of the policies IN it (the documenter's platform strings, carried on every compared policy since birth). Narrows the list; the verdict cards keep counting the whole scan, said in the control's own title. Offered only when the scan spans more than one platform. — v1.0.3 (build 10523): THE WARM START — opening the scan runs it over the shared cache when one is held: the detection is local arithmetic, so a warm open costs NO read at all, and the answer says which read it scanned ('Scanned over the sign-in read at HH:MM'). The cache is the whole collection; the tool takes its three surfaces of it, keeping the resolver, filter names and group counts by reference — one collection, three views. The Markdown export prints 'tenant read …' beside 'generated' when the two differ. ⚔️ Scan the tenant stays the fresh read. — BETA — v0.2 (build 10418) applies the 10413 layout, last of the four: stat cards for the verdicts (can collide, may, cannot, settings set by more than one policy, surfaces unread when it applies), and each conflict is a FOLDED row — closed, the setting, the verdict badge and the policy count; open, the side-by-side grid the finding exists for, every policy with its value and its reach tags. Open set keyed on section|setting, surviving re-renders. Every verdict caveat, the redaction rule and the unread-surfaces honesty unchanged. Original:  R20, after Alper Atar's IntuneShade (MIT): the same setting configured to different values in two policies, found by IDENTITY, never display name — definition id in the settings catalog, definition+category in ADMX, typed property WITHIN one policy type for device configurations; a cross-surface collision on the same CSP is stated as NOT DETECTED rather than guessed, because matching a legacy typed property to a catalog definition would be display-name matching wearing a canonical hat (a departure from the card's first draft, said on the card). THE READ IS T05's collect() — same surfaces, same redaction, same failure honesty; a second copy of thirteen-surface reading is how two tools start disagreeing about one tenant. One step past the original: a collision is only a conflict IF THE POLICIES CAN MEET ON A DEVICE — verdicts computed from assignments: can collide (shared included group, or tenant-wide meeting reach), may collide (different groups CAN share members; an assignment filter caps any verdict at may — the filter id now rides on Docs.assignmentOf for exactly this), cannot collide ONLY where one side reaches nobody by construction. Agreement is not a finding; redacted values are never compared (two secrets are not known to be equal); unread surfaces are named as unknown, not clean. Findings, not a score. Reads only. Exports MD + CSV" },
  toolAssignEdit: { t: 11, v: "1.0.10", note: "v1.0.10 (build 10522): THE PLATFORM FILTER — T05's select beside the text filter: Windows, macOS, iOS/iPadOS, Android, Linux, Not platform-specific, each with its count over the whole list. Platforms are derived from the RAW object through Docs.platformsOf (both list paths now carry raw), memoised per row — never a second parse of @odata.type here. Narrows the LIST only: the tick-set lives outside the DOM (10390) so the selection survives the platform filter exactly as it survives the text filter, and the rail counts stay whole-tenant. — v1.0.9 (build 10521): THE HANDOFF — AssignEditTool.openWith(sectionId, policyId): another tool hands a policy over and the editor opens with it selected, through the tile's own handler (the 10398 click-through rule — crumb, tab and sidebar follow). A handoff into a cold list WAITS for the list (pendingSelect, consumed by the warm start or the fresh read, whichever builds it first), and a policy gone since the other tool read is silently not ticked — a tick would lie. canEdit(sectionId) is the one home of the section→surface mapping, SEC_OF read the other way. First caller: T19's popout actions. — v1.0.8 (build 10520): THE WARM START — opening the tool lists the policies when the shared cache holds them (its four surfaces are four of T05's thirteen, consumed RAW via keepRaw because the write pipeline works on untouched assignment targets). The list says where it came from and when, in the render so it survives re-renders. ✏️ Read the policies stays the FRESH read — a plan is cut from the newest list the tenant will give — and every write still re-reads per policy (the drift check), so a stale row can never become a stale write. After an apply the shared cache is invalidated: it described the tenant before the write. — v1.0.7 (build 10519): A POLICY NAME OPENS THE \ud83d\udcc4 DOCUMENTER'S POPOUT \u2014 Docs.popoutHtml, the one template T05, T19 and T20 already share, with T19's foot (Close). The read is Docs.collect() narrowed to the ONE policy (`only`, added to collect in this build), so settings, named groups with member counts and named filters are exactly what the documenter prints \u2014 not a summary that could drift. The tick box stays the selection; the name is the look inside (.pname, the existing hover affordance). Cached per read, cleared on a fresh read \u2014 a popout served from before the last read would show assignments the apply step just changed. Directory read rides along at the click for the group names, T19's own union. A policy deleted since the list was read is an error card, not an empty popout. \u2014 v0.6 (build 10494): THE NOOP CHECK SEES THE FILTER \u2014 it compared target type and group id only, so an unfiltered include and a filtered one were called the same assignment and a write silently did nothing; same group with a different filter is now refused and named. BETA — v0.4 (build 10404, Toolkit parity): TENANT-WIDE TARGETS and ASSIGNMENT FILTERS join the bar. The target seg offers Group / All devices / All users — Graph's only two tenant-wide targets; the plan header for one reads THE WHOLE TENANT, now and every device or user it gains later, and add-exclude on a tenant-wide target is REFUSED because Graph has no tenant-wide exclusion (an exclusion names a group). A filter dropdown (fed by T14's own Filters.list — same scope, one request, a tenant without filters keeps No filter) puts an assignment filter with include/exclude mode on ADDS only: a removal takes the whole assignment, filter included, and removing-just-the-filter would be an edit wearing a removal's name. The groups read scope now rides the read-policies consent, so the group box suggests from the tenant from the first keystroke. v0.3 (build 10400): the operation moves to a FLOATING SELECTION BAR at the bottom — ENCA's selbar, floated — that exists exactly while something is ticked: count, operation seg, group input and dry run travel with the selection, a ✕ clears it and the bar goes with it, and the list keeps its full height. Chosen over a sticky top bar in a mockup round. The bar lives inside the tool's own section so leaving the tool hides it, and its left edge tracks the content column's formula in both sidebar states. v0.2 (build 10390): the Intune Toolkit's own shape at last — SURFACES ON THE LEFT (a sticky rail with per-surface counts and tick counts, All on top, unreadable surfaces named under it), FILTER ON TOP, the list beside. And the fix that made the rail honest: THE SELECTION LIVES IN JS, NOT THE DOM — it used to be read off the checkboxes, so every filter keystroke re-rendered the table and silently dropped every tick, and a surface rail would have made cross-surface picking impossible. Checkboxes render FROM the set and write INTO it, picked() never looks at the DOM, ticking never re-renders the table (scroll position survives bulk ticking), the rail's ✓ counts follow live, and a fresh read clears the set because a fresh read is a fresh decision. — bulk assignment editing: add an include, add an exclusion, or remove a group across many policies at once, the thing the portal makes you do one blade at a time. After Maxime Guillemin's Intune-Toolkit (MIT), whose surface map and question this is; THE WRITE DISCIPLINE IS TUNO'S OWN, because this is the second thing in TUNO that writes and the first that writes AT SCALE — the mistake it enables is not a duplicate policy but a policy reaching the wrong population, invisible until somebody's laptop behaves differently. FOUR SURFACES, ALL UNDER THE ONE WRITE SCOPE THE REGISTRATION ALREADY DECLARES: device configurations, settings catalog, compliance, administrative templates. Scripts and applications are deliberately absent — each would be a NEW write scope, and adding one is a decision to take in the open (the R18 rule), not a feature's side effect. THE PIPELINE IS THE TOOL: read → plan → backup → confirm → apply → verify, enforced by the screen. The dry run lists EVERY operation with the group's NAME and MEMBER COUNT (an empty group is warned as configuring nothing), noops are listed as noops, and two operations are REFUSED outright with T09's reasoning: an include onto a policy that excludes the group would create the include+exclude contradiction, and an exclusion onto a policy that includes it is a contradiction, not a removal — remove the include instead. The backup (every affected policy's current assignments, as a restorable file) is taken automatically and applying stays locked until it is; removals are confirmed separately from additions, by TYPING the group's name. /assign REPLACES the whole list, so untouched assignments are re-serialised to exactly the fields the API takes — FILTERS INCLUDED, because dropping a filter on an untouched assignment would silently widen it. Every write is preceded by a FRESH read: a policy whose assignments changed since the plan was made is skipped as DRIFTED rather than overwritten, because replacing a list somebody else just edited is how two admins erase each other. Every write is followed by a read-back, and 'verified' is the tenant's word, not the request's status code. Writes never retry; sequential, never pooled; stop-on-first-failure on by default. NO DELETE, NO RENAME — the Toolkit has both behind its own Advanced Actions toggle and neither belongs in a browser tool whose whole argument is that it cannot do much harm. The write scope is asked for at the apply click and never before. After Maxime Guillemin's Intune Toolkit (MIT)" },
  toolSetSearch: { t: 10, v: "1.0.1", note: "BETA — 'which policies configure BitLocker, and to what value?', answered from the setting DEFINITIONS rather than the policy names. The last piece of Ugur Koc's IntuneAssignmentChecker (MIT) worth having. T05 searches the settings of policies it has already read, which finds a setting only if you already know which policy holds it; this searches the other way round, against the settings-catalog definition catalog — roughly seventeen thousand definitions, which are Microsoft's and not the tenant's. TWO READS, DELIBERATELY SEPARATE: the catalog once per session, held in memory and searched as you type with ranked token matching (name-starts beats name-contains beats keyword beats description, every token must land somewhere, and an empty query is no results rather than seventeen thousand); then, optionally, the tenant's own settings-catalog policies with their settings — the same N+1 the backup pays, pooled — so each result can say WHO sets it and TO WHAT. UNTIL THAT SECOND READ RUNS THE USAGE COLUMN IS ABSENT, NOT EMPTY, and the screen and the export both say so, because 'nobody sets this' and 'nobody looked' are different answers. Child settings are indexed under their own definition ids, so a definition configured as somebody's choice-child is still found. VALUES PASS THE DOCUMENTER'S REDACTION GATE — Docs.redactValue, the same implementation, not a copy — because a settings search is exactly the tool somebody uses to hunt for 'password', and a result table printing secret values would be a disclosure engine with a search box. A policy whose settings could not be read is named, and its absence from the usage column is stated rather than silent. Reads only, one scope. After Ugur Koc's Intune Assignment Checker (MIT)" },
  toolHealth: { t: 9, v: "1.0.3", note: "v1.0.3 (build 10542, the layout round): STICKY CHIPS (T19's fix) \u2014 the kind filter gains a pinned compact face above the findings: the same filter as the cards, reachable after two hundred folds. The All chip clears. BETA — v0.2 (build 10416) applies the 10413 layout: SIX STAT CARDS, one per finding kind, that double as FILTERS — click a kind to see only it, click again for everything, with the failing card saying NOT CHECKED rather than a reassuring zero when status was off; the five stacked tables become ONE flat list of FOLDED findings, badge saying the kind, unfolding in place to the evidence — which group, which id, what the finding means and what it does not — with the open set keyed on stable finding keys so it survives both re-renders and the kind filter. Original:  the assignments that exist and reach nobody, in one place, every finding the same shape of problem: something that looks configured and is not. After Ugur Koc's IntuneAssignmentChecker (MIT), whose idea this is — an assignment reaching an empty group is a FINDING, not a row. SIX FINDING KINDS: assignments into EMPTY groups, checked transitively with a one-row peek rather than a count because 'is there at least one member' is the whole question and a $top=1 page batches where the text/plain count endpoint does not; DANGLING references, distinct from empty because a dangling id names a group the directory no longer has while an empty group still exists and simply holds nobody — different fault, same symptom, both listed; UNASSIGNED policies, which T05 can filter for and this treats as a finding, with enrolment restrictions deliberately exempt because their built-in defaults are unassigned by design and flagging them would bury the real rows; EXCLUDED-ONLY policies, where every assignment is an exclusion and there is no include to carve from; one group both INCLUDED AND EXCLUDED on the same policy, where the exclusion wins and the include reads as reach it does not deliver; and optionally FAILED DEPLOYMENTS, from the cheap per-policy status Graph keeps for device configurations, compliance policies, scripts and remediations. THE STATUS COVERAGE IS PARTIAL AND SAYS SO: settings catalog, ADMX, apps and app protection keep their status behind the reports API and are named as UNCHECKED rather than passed off as healthy, because a health page that silently checks only some surfaces reads as a clean bill for the rest. FINDINGS, NOT A SCORE — whether an empty group is a mistake or a landing zone prepared for next quarter is not something this tool can know. A resolveNames failure is UNKNOWN, never 'all dangling'; an unpeekable group is unknown, not empty; a failed surface is unknown, not clean. The unassigned detection rides GroupUse.intuneHits as an opt-in flag, keyed on the assignments array being empty rather than on no hits matching, so T02/T06/T08 are unchanged. Assignment reads via GroupUse.SOURCES. Reads only. After Ugur Koc's Intune Assignment Checker (MIT)" },
  toolWhatIf: { t: 8, v: "1.0.2", note: "v1.0.2 (build 10540, the layout round): THE RAIL \u2014 Gained | Lost | Pre-excluded | Unchanged, one delta table at a time, so the Unchanged table (routinely the longest and the least asked for) stops burying the three tables anyone actually wants. The summary strip, the simulation-not-a-promise note and the failed-surfaces line stay on every pane; an empty pane says so; compare mode is one table and stays as it was. BETA — what would change if this user or device joined, or left, that group: the question asked while a change is still cheap to reverse, and the one capability of Ugur Koc's IntuneAssignmentChecker that TUNO had nothing like. THE ENDPOINT LIST IS NOT A SECOND COPY: assignments are read through GroupUse.SOURCES, T06's rule, because the first time a surface is added to one list and not the other this tool starts reporting 'nothing changes' about a workload it never looked at. HOW THE SIMULATION WORKS is on the screen rather than hidden, because an oracle gets trusted past its competence: memberships today are read transitively (users from /users, devices from the ENTRA device object — the Intune record cannot say which groups a machine is in), the hypothetical set is built — JOINING adds the group AND everything it is transitively a member of, because membership of a child is membership of its ancestors; LEAVING severs the direct edge and rebuilds the closure from what remains, because a parent reachable through another group is NOT lost — and every surface is read ONCE with the sweep's null match set, each policy's effect computed under both sets. FOUR DELTA STATES rather than two: gained, lost (including included-to-excluded, because JOINING A GROUP CAN TAKE POLICY AWAY — the most surprising row this tool produces), pre-excluded (no change today, but the subject cannot receive the policy later while the exclusion stands), unchanged. An app whose intent is uninstall and starts to apply says WILL BE UNINSTALLED rather than gained. A membership held only through nesting cannot be resigned from and the answer says so instead of simulating an impossible action; a dynamic group cannot be joined by hand and the answer is labelled conditional on the rule. A SIMULATION, NOT A PROMISE: filtered assignments stay 'may' in both directions, tenant-wide targets are not in the delta at all because they apply whatever the groups say, and a leave whose closure could not be fully expanded is called a FLOOR rather than an answer. Compare mode puts two to four groups side by side — each column the group plus its own parents — and shows only where they differ. Reads only; no write scope is reachable from the file. After Ugur Koc's Intune Assignment Checker (MIT)" },
  toolAppLocker: { t: 1, v: "1.0.28", note: "v1.0.28 (build 10516): THE VERSION TOKEN IS A 72px BOX AGAIN. In \u00a75 Get it into Intune it is an inline field in the middle of a sentence \u2014 \u201c\u2026 - R27.1 - V[4.0.2], grouping \u2026\u201d \u2014 and it had been rendering 981px wide since build 10466, wrapping that sentence into three lines around itself. Not this tool's bug: 10466 made the field look app-wide and scoped it to `main` with five :not() clauses, and five :not()s weigh as much as five classes, so .al-dep-ver's width:72px could never win. Fixed in the stylesheet for every tool at once (see the TUNO note), measured on the deployed beta before and after: 981px \u2192 72px. \u2014 0.27: loop marks belong to ONE tenant. A hand-marked station is a claim — Deploy audit · marked by you, ticked against tenant A, is not true of tenant B — so the marks now carry the tenant id they were made under (the 10457 organization read) and clear themselves the first time the strip renders under a different tenant. Marks made signed out are adopted by the first tenant that signs in, the pre-10460 storage shape migrates the same way, and a browser that never signs in keeps the old behaviour whole. The ☐ tooltip says so.;0.26: iterating stops being a portal chore — UPDATE IN PLACE and an editable version at the button (mockup round, option A). The version token in the create row is now an input: edit it, it moves in the NAME only, the grouping never follows, junk is refused. And the same-grouping stop grew the one deliberate exception to never-overwrite: ✎ Update it in place opens a confirm card naming the deployed profile and the promise — TUNO writes the adjusted rules and the new version name INTO the deployed profile, same grouping, same object, so its assignments never move and there is nothing to unassign; removals land because the omaSettings array is replaced whole. It re-reads the tenant before writing, keeps the DEPLOYED grouping (that is what makes it an edit instead of a twin), and is also reachable from the 🔎 check button's grouping-match line — because a draft with open findings has its deploy disabled, which is exactly when someone is iterating. Supersede (new profile + moved assignments) was mocked and declined: it has a transition window and two-profiles-one-grouping sequencing for no gain over the in-place edit.;0.25: a finding whose fix has been taken SAYS SO. Applying the hash-rules fix left the unsigned-executables finding sitting there unchanged — same three files, same recommendation, button gone — which read, verbatim, as the fix returning to its previous state and not working any more. The rules had landed (Rules card) and the ✔ Applied notice had faded after a few seconds; the finding was the only witness left on screen and it was still telling yesterday's story. Now, when every unsigned artifact's collection carries its TUNO hash rule, the finding changes state: Info, COVERED by the hash rules in this draft, with the standing duty written in (hash rules die on the file's next update — calendar the expiry) and the way back named (delete the TUNO hash rules in the Rules card and the Medium finding with its fix reopens). Undo restores the original finding, fix and all. The Add-default-Windows-rules buttons were never gone — right column of the Enforcement per collection table, showing ✓ present where the defaults already exist.;0.24: the grouping explains itself where the deploy happens, and the deployed identity is one click away. A standing line at the create button says what the grouping IS — the policy's address on the device; same policy next iteration → same grouping, edit in place; a new GUID deploys a second policy BESIDE the old one and the device merges both, so removals never land. Next to it, 🔎 Check against the tenant (same read the deploy uses, asked at the click, reachable even while findings block the deploy button — which is exactly when someone is iterating): it lists any AppLocker profile deployed under a DIFFERENT grouping and offers ⤓ Adopt identity — the deployed profile's grouping plus its name at the NEXT version land in the form while the ADJUSTED draft on the table stays untouched, unlike the events card's full pull which replaces the draft. Re-checking then confirms the match, and the collision stop-box explains that a same-grouping stop while iterating is the system working: take the Intune tab's values into the portal, same grouping, edited in place.;0.23: Apply is never a dead button. The hash-rules fix deduplicates by rule name, so applying it twice added nothing the second time — and said nothing, which read as broken (the bug report, verbatim). Now a fix whose rules already exist is not offered at all, and any confirm-card Apply that ends up adding nothing says why in the card — the rules already exist from an earlier apply; delete them in the Rules card to rebuild — instead of dying silently.;0.22: a fleet-gap FLOOD collapses to one expandable finding. Adding a handful of DLL hash allows put rules in the Dll collection, and one rule in a collection makes AppLocker enforce the WHOLE collection — so every other DLL load woke as a would-block and the findings table became two hundred rows of scroll. Real, but unreadable. More than eight gaps in one collection now collapse to a single High finding: the count, the why (rules enforce the whole collection), a recommendation that says allowing DLLs one hash at a time is not a policy — decide whether Dll should be governed, or remove its rules and the events return to set-aside — and every file in an expand/collapse list. A handful of gaps keep their individual rows and confirm-card fixes. Long unbroken file names also stopped crushing the rule column (overflow-wrap).;0.21: THREE CARDS, ONE PER LAYOUT — the single merged card put the findings table, the coverage table and the rule tables under one roof, and three different layouts in one card read as a broken card. The split follows the layouts: Findings (decisions first), Microsoft app coverage, Rules with their findings still nested. Everything the merges won stays won — one fix flow, nested findings, sticky headers now one per card with the ✔ Applied notice landing in whichever is pinned — and the empty leftover host cards are gone. The gap report now ALWAYS carries a Microsoft app coverage section: the change-ticket document answers can-a-standard-user-still-run-OneDrive in the same envelope as the gaps, and a report generated without a policy says so instead of omitting the section silently.;0.20: five corrections from one real working session, all in the merged card. The header is STICKY — the long card always says whose card you are scrolled into. Microsoft app coverage folded in as a section (its old card is empty; the Add-allow-rule fix rides along). The events finding CARRIES the audited-and-blocked list it promises — expandable in place, aggregated per file with counts and users, instead of a below that pointed at nothing. ONE FIX FLOW EVERYWHERE: the fleet-gap and unsigned-hash fixes open the same confirm card the rule editors use, showing the exact rule before Apply commits it — two different fix behaviours on one screen read as one of them broken. Every applied fix announces itself: ✔ Applied in the sticky header with Undo beside it, fading after a few seconds; and the publisher editor refuses an unchanged Apply with the reason (setting no upper bound used to rewrite the rule identically and read as a fix that did nothing). Plus: the unsigned-in-writable-locations finding gained its own fix — one hash rule per collection, every hash inside, the expiry warning written into the rule.;0.19: RULES AND FINDINGS ARE ONE CARD (mockup round, option A). A finding that is ABOUT a rule renders nested under that rule inside the per-collection rule tables — severity, source mark, reason, condition when it adds something, recommendation and the fix button, with the judgement editors opening in place; findings about nothing in particular (missing collections, enforcement judgements, scan directory verdicts without a rule, fleet gaps) keep the table shape at the top. The compact narrow-column summary still covers every finding; the rules stay visible in the narrow column where the table folds. One scroll instead of two over one story: here is a rule, and here is what is wrong with it. The old Rules card is empty; the sev filter chips and the whole fix framework carry over untouched — a nested fix is the same fix in a different seat.;0.18: ONE TABLE. The fleet gaps became FINDINGS — 📡-marked rows in the findings table beside the audit's and the scan's, closed through the same fix framework (publisher/hash/exact-path per the event's own evidence, mutate/undo, one click) — and the evidence card shrank to a summary: facts, one counts line naming gaps/by-design/covered/undecided/DLL-set-aside with a link into Findings, the gap report, the tenant-pull chooser, warnings. Two tables were saying the same kind of thing about the same files, which was the overview being long; covered and by-design need no rows on screen — they are counts here and sections in the gap report. The 10436 filter chips retired with the second table.;0.17: DLL events are SET ASIDE, not undecided. Generated policies omit the DLL collection deliberately — absence is the only state that restricts nothing — so every DLL load in the fleet log is the record of that decision, not a question. They leave the default view and the undecided count, get their own chip (N DLL — set aside) that brings them back with the why written in the row, take no fix button, and the gap report carries them as their own section. A draft that CARRIES DLL rules classifies them normally, because then somebody chose to police DLL loads on purpose.;0.16: the evidence chips became FILTERS (T09's cards-as-filters): click 4 gaps to close and the table shows exactly those four — the 50-row cap applies after the filter, the active chip reads as pressed, clicking it again drops the filter, and the gap report deliberately ignores it because a change-ticket document covers everything. A ✕ Clear on the card drops just the events evidence (the policy stays); a 🧹 Start over in the step-2 toolbar empties the whole table — policy, scan, evidence, tenant-pull state, manual loop marks — behind a confirm whose one promise is that nothing in the tenant is touched. The upload button now names all three files it takes: scan bundle · policy XML · events results.;0.15: events-first is a first-class entrance. The mid-loop return carries ONLY the events bundle — the policy lives in the TENANT — and the old flow answered that moment with an alert telling people to hunt for a file. The evidence card now lives OUTSIDE the policy-only body, renders the moment a bundle lands (no alert), and leads with the actual question: what to judge it against. Two answers on the card: upload the draft (same button), or PULL THE DEPLOYED APPLOCKER PROFILE FROM THE TENANT — list the custom profiles, keep only those carrying AppLocker OMA-URIs, click one and the policy is rebuilt from its RuleCollection values. Pulling adopts the profile NAME and GROUPING into the Intune form, so a later export edits the same profile in place and the deploy panel refuses to create a twin beside it, which is exactly right. Values Graph withholds are refused with a reason, never guessed.;0.14: the loop strip takes manual marks — a small ☐ on every station the session cannot verify (the portal edit above all). Three honesty rules: EVIDENCE BEATS THE MARK both ways (an auto-lit station ignores it, and Gaps with open gaps stays amber and offers no toggle at all), a hand-marked station renders DASHED with marked-by-you so a claim never dresses as evidence, and marks persist per browser because the portal edit done yesterday is still done after a refresh. The collapsed summary shows a starred tick (✓*) for hand-marks.;0.13: the audit loop strip — the page reads 1→5 top to bottom but the work is a circle, and now the circle is drawn. Seven stations above step 1 (Scan → Build → Deploy audit → Collect → Gaps → Update profile → Enforce), each lit from what is actually loaded or known to be in the tenant THIS session, never from belief: the scan bundle lights Scan with the device name, rules on the table light Build, a created-or-found audit profile lights Deploy, an uploaded events bundle lights Collect, the Gaps station carries the live open-gap count (amber while open, green at zero — same classification pass as the chips and the gap report, so the numbers cannot disagree), and the Enforce station reads the existing gate. You-are-here is the first station not done, which is also the answer to what-next; clicking a station scrolls to its part of the page. The Update station is the honest one: a portal edit is invisible to a browser tab, and the strip says so instead of pretending. Collapses to a one-line summary, remembered per browser. All colors are theme variables — light, dark and auto for free. Mockup round: live strip chosen over a Help-only walkthrough.;0.12: the fleet evidence card grows teeth — a GAP REPORT and fixes that close what it finds. Every denied file in an uploaded events bundle is classified against the draft on screen: GAP (would still be blocked, machine space — needs a decision before enforcing), BLOCKED BY DESIGN (user-writable origin — the policy working), COVERED (the draft already allows it — the block belonged to the old policy), or UNDECIDED (no rules for the type). The chips count all rows, not just the 50 shown, and ⭳ Gap report downloads the same judgement as Markdown for the change ticket — summary table, per-section detail, suggested fix per gap, the collector's own blind spots included. Each gap row carries a 🔧 fix that adds the rule to the draft through the normal mutate/undo path: PUBLISHER when the event carries a signer, HASH from the event's FileHash when not (0x-normalised; SourceFileLength 0 and the rule's description says why, and that hash rules go stale), exact PATH as the last resort with instructions to replace it — never a directory, because a directory allow from event evidence rebuilds the hole. By-design rows get the same mechanics behind a cooler label (Allow anyway) — closing one is a business decision and the button reads like one. The event-verdict matcher also learned hashes: events carry a FileHash where coverage artifacts do not, so a hash-rule fix flips its row to covered instead of reading as a gap forever.;0.11: the AppControl events-collection set joins the toolkit — Get-TunoAppControlEvents.ps1 (the evidence pump: CodeIntegrity + all four AppLocker logs into per-ID CSV/XML, an HTML report, and a tuno.applocker.events JSON bundle, all landing in the IME Logs folder named .log so Collect diagnostics gathers them), Detect-TunoAppControlEvents.ps1 (always exits 1 on purpose — the remediation IS the harvest), and Compress-TunoAppControlReport.ps1 (zips report + bundle for MDE Live Response getfile). Downloadable in step 1, deployable from the browser as the THIRD Remediation pair, and — the point — the bundle is importable: upload AppControlEvents_Bundle_*.log and every blocked or audited fleet event is matched against the draft on screen with the same matcher the coverage table uses, aggregated per file with a recommendation each: covered (the block belonged to the old policy), stays-blocked-by-design (user-writable origin — the policy working), or missing-rule (signed, machine space — add a publisher rule). Replaces the CloudFlows Remedate_ACB set with three defects fixed: MSI/Script IDs 8005/8007 were never collected so an enforced MSI block left no evidence, a global SilentlyContinue made every catch dead code, and the 48-ID CodeIntegrity query exceeded the ~23-comparison XPath limit and silently returned nothing.;0.10: the IT-TOOLS house-folder provisioning ships as a detect-and-remediate pair — Detect-TunoItToolsFolders.ps1 (new, 1.0.0) beside Initialize-TunoItToolsFolders.ps1 (1.1.0, which now PROVES SYSTEM can write by appending a provisioning record to IT-TOOLS\\LOGS, where the house scripts log), downloadable in step 1 and deployable from the browser as a second Remediation next to the AppLocker cleanup pair. Detection reports missing folders, writes open to anyone outside SYSTEM/Administrators, and SYSTEM itself unable to write — evaluated with the same write mask and trusted set the remediation verifies with, so the pair cannot loop. Unlike the cleanup pair this one is a STANDING assignment: leave it scheduled, it is the guard on the ACL the standing allow rules depend on.;0.9: BETA — AppLocker policy builder & validator, now an end-to-end workflow rather than an XML reader: scan → upload → review → export → deploy. (1) SCAN. The tool hands out Invoke-TunoAppLockerScan.ps1, served from the site so the copy downloaded always matches the build. It is a modern reimplementation of Microsoft AaronLocker's approach — native DACL evaluation instead of Sysinternals AccessChk, Deny ACEs honoured, JSON instead of Excel COM, and it runs on PowerShell 5.1 AND 7 where AaronLocker hard-fails on anything but 5.1. It finds every directory a non-administrator can write to, inventories the executables in them with signer/version/hash, reads the AppLocker event logs, and builds a publisher-first rule set with every writable directory injected as an exception. (2) UPLOAD. The same import button takes the scan's JSON bundle or a plain policy XML; the bundle carries the generated Audit and Enforce policies plus the device's own effective policy, and any of the three can be put on the table. (3) REVIEW. The static check set is joined by scan-derived findings marked 🛰 — a writable directory an allow rule still reaches, unsigned binaries that can only ever get hash rules, executions the endpoint has already refused — reached with the SAME evaluator as the Microsoft coverage table by probing a hypothetical executable dropped into the directory. (4) EXPORT. The code panel gained a second tab: the Intune custom profile (windows10CustomConfiguration, one OMA-URI per collection, the DLL collection OMITTED), built from the same serialiser as the XML. NotConfigured is never written into a generated policy: Microsoft's own guidance is that a rule collection in that state CARRYING RULES is enforced, so shipping DLL rules that way would have enforced DLL control rather than parking it. Absence is the only inert state, and the audit reports a NotConfigured collection with rules as High \u2014 it is blocking today while reading as inactive. (5) DEPLOY, from the browser. Step 5 creates the profile in the tenant you are signed in to, under rules narrower than Graph would allow: the write scope is asked for on the click and never at sign-in; a read runs first and a profile with the same name or the same AppLocker grouping STOPS the deploy rather than being overwritten; assignment is a second, separate act that names the pilot group and its member count before it does anything; and the Enforce profile stays locked until the audit profile exists in that tenant AND an uploaded scan shows nothing blocked and nothing that would have been. Convert-TunoAppLockerToIntune.ps1 remains for the command line, standalone with no customer-connection harness, offline JSON or -Online via Connect-MgGraph. Still runs entirely in the browser; nothing is uploaded. ENFORCEMENT IS NEVER AUTOMATIC: the only mode change the tool makes on its own is the one that blocks nothing. AuditOnly to Enabled opens a judgement panel listing what should be true first (time in audit across a month-end, a patch cycle and a new starter; an event log with nothing unrecognised left; a coverage table reading allowed; a tested way back) and the admin picks the mode; adding an absent collection lands in AuditOnly. The DLL panel states what enforcing DLL costs, matching the scanner and the export. The publisher check no longer fires its breadth heuristics on rules naming BOTH a publisher and a product \u2014 the shape the coverage fixes build \u2014 so the tool stopped flagging the rule it had just recommended. After Spencer Alessi's AppLockerInspector and Microsoft's AaronLocker" },
  toolDocs: { t: 5, v: "1.0.12", note: "v1.0.12 (build 10523): THE WARM START — opening the documenter shows the tenant when the shared cache holds it: browse face ready, everything selected, exports live, with the source line above the list. AND EVERY EXPORT NAMES THE READ IT DESCRIBES: meta gains `read` (the tenant-read time, from res.readAt) and all three renderers print 'tenant read …' beside 'generated' whenever the two moments differ — 'generated now' must not launder a cached read into a fresh-looking file. 📄 Read the tenant stays the fresh read of the chosen sections. One landing function for both paths (landRes), so they cannot drift in what they reset. — v1.0.11 (build 10522): platformsOf CLAIMS WINDOWS FOR ADMX — an administrative template is Windows group policy by definition, and 'not platform-specific' was the normaliser's gap, not the policy's claim (autopilot's own rule, extended). Found when T11's new platform filter counted one Windows policy in a two-policy fixture. T05's platform filter now files ADMX under Windows too — the one-normaliser rule working as intended. — v1.0.10 (build 10520): collect() TAKES `keepRaw` — the raw Graph objects ride beside the mapped items, for the shared policy cache: T11's write pipeline needs untouched assignment targets, and a cache holding only the mapped shape could never feed it. Memory only; nothing renders or exports the raw copies. — v1.0.9 (build 10519): collect() TAKES `only` — a section's items narrowed to a single id right after the list read, so the detail N+1, the naming and the member counts run for exactly one policy. Added for T11's popout; the alternative was a second single-policy mapper, which is how two tools start disagreeing about one tenant (the T12 rule). Nothing changes for callers that do not pass it. — v1.0.8 (build 10505): EVERY GROUP CHIP CARRIES ITS MEMBER COUNT. collect() pools Graph.memberCount beside the group NAMES \u2014 one read for the whole collection, the pattern the names already used \u2014 and assignmentText appends it, so the popout, the Markdown, the HTML report and the Word export all say \"SG-Pilot (Included) \u00b7 27 members\" without four copies of the arithmetic. An empty group says 0 members ON PURPOSE (that is \ud83e\ude7a Assignment health's finding, worth seeing where the target is named); a group whose count could not be read prints NOTHING rather than a zero. The filter read also gains `rule`, which is what lets the reach arithmetic in T20 stop saying \"at most\" about a target it can count. v1.0.7: v0.7 (build 10492): the browse row NAMES the assignment rather than counting it, the search reaches group and filter names (T19 had done this since it shipped, over the same collection), and the disabled search box says why it is disabled instead of leaving that to a paragraph. v0.6 (build 10490): the assignment-filters read asked for DeviceManagementRBAC.Read.All where Microsoft documents DeviceManagementConfiguration.Read.All \u2014 wrong since the section was written, and 10482 built filter naming on top of it; Docs.filterOfTarget() becomes the house\u0027s one reader of a filter off a raw target, keyed on the id rather than the type. v0.5 (build 10489): ONE PHRASE FOR ONE ASSIGNMENT \u2014 the popout, the Markdown, the HTML report and the Word export had four copies of name (kind) and every one dropped the assignment filter and printed \u0022All devices \u00b7 All devices\u0022; a filtered All-devices target had been exporting as whole-fleet reach in the document an auditor reads. BETA \u2014 thirteen configuration surfaces read once, then browsed and written down. TWO VIEWS OVER ONE READ, the T03 shape: BROWSE is the tool you use on a Tuesday \u2014 every policy in the tenant in one filterable list, settings expanded underneath, assignments resolved to group names \u2014 and DOCUMENT is the artefact an auditor asks for and nobody has. They are one tool because reading thirteen surfaces twice would be absurd: you browse what you are about to document. Settings catalog, device configurations, administrative templates, compliance, app protection, app configuration, scripts and remediations, Windows update profiles, enrolment, Autopilot, applications, assignment filters and scope tags. Exports Markdown for a wiki, a standalone HTML page that prints cleanly, and a Word document for the auditor who will edit it \u2014 with real Word tables rather than a run of paragraphs somebody has to reformat. FILTERS APPLY TO THE EXPORTS, and every export header states how many of how many objects it holds, so a filtered document cannot be mistaken for a complete one. REDACTION IS NOT A SETTING: a tenant document is the most widely circulated thing this tool produces, and a script body, certificate, pre-shared key or encrypted OMA-URI value in one is a disclosure that cannot be recalled \u2014 every such field is replaced with a marker recording that a value exists without carrying it, and there is deliberately no switch. A section that could not be read is named as unreadable rather than silently absent; a policy whose settings could not be read is still LISTED, with the gap stated, because unlike a backup a document that admits what it does not know is more useful than one missing the object entirely. Coverage after Ugur Koc's IntuneDocumentation, which is licensed Elastic 2.0 \u2014 reimplemented from its published coverage list, not forked, and sharing none of its code. That project also has a server tier its browser hands a token to; TUNO has nowhere to put one, so the collection runs in the tab, which is the more literal version of the claim. Reads only. v0.2: clicking a policy opens a POPOUT rather than expanding it in place \u2014 a settings-catalog policy has twenty-odd rows, so opening one in the list pushed everything else off the screen and closing it lost your place; ENCA's modal, same classes. And policies are SELECTABLE, with the exports following the selection rather than the filter: the filter narrows what you are looking at, the tick boxes decide what you are sending, and keeping them apart is what lets you filter to find three policies, tick them, clear the filter and still export three. Everything is selected after a read because the common case is 'document the tenant'; select all, select what is shown, invert and select none sit in a bar above the list, and a whole section can be ticked from its heading. With nothing selected the export buttons disappear \u2014 a button that would produce an empty document is worse than no button. Every export header now distinguishes A SELECTION from A FILTERED VIEW, because 'I picked these twelve' and 'a platform filter left twelve' are different claims and neither is 'this is the tenant'. v0.3 fixes the platform filter, which had nothing usable in it. Graph names the same platform three ways depending on which surface you ask \u2014 a settings-catalog policy says 'windows10', a device configuration implies 'Windows' through its type \u2014 and the first version passed whatever it found straight into the list, so a tenant with both offered TWO ENTRIES FOR ONE PLATFORM, each matching half the estate. A policy targeting two platforms arrived as the single opaque string 'windows10,macOS' and matched neither. Graph's literal 'none' appeared as though it were a platform. Everything normalises to one vocabulary now \u2014 Windows, macOS, iOS/iPadOS, Android, Linux \u2014 a policy carries a LIST because it really can target more than one, and a policy with no platform at all (a script, an assignment filter, a scope tag) is filterable as 'Not platform-specific' rather than being invisible in every filtered view. The filters are also switched off until a read, with a line saying why: the list is built from what the tenant returns and cannot exist before then, and an empty control with no explanation is exactly how this looked broken. v0.4 makes the list FIXED: all five platforms every time, plus the not-platform-specific bucket, each carrying its count. Building it from what the read returned sounded tidier and was worse in practice \u2014 the options changed shape from tenant to tenant, a single-platform estate got a control with one entry and nothing to do, and an admin who believes they have no Linux had no way to confirm it. A zero is an answer that an absent entry cannot give. The counts deliberately do not sum to the total, because a policy targeting two platforms is counted under both" },
  toolBackup: { t: 4, v: "1.0.4", note: "v1.0.4 (build 10524): THE RESTORE PICKER FILTERS ON PLATFORM — the documenter's platform reading over the archived raw objects ('restore only the macOS profiles'). The filter toggles row VISIBILITY and never re-renders, because the ticks and the edited names in that table are DOM state a re-render would silently throw away; a hidden ticked row still restores, and the control's title says so. An area whose every object is hidden folds its header away. Objects declaring no platform (scripts, filters, the non-policy areas) file under Not platform-specific. —v0.3 (build 10426, R15 after Ugur Koc's TenuVault TUI, MIT) WIDENS AND HARDENS THE ARCHIVE. Nineteen export-only areas join the interop five — intents, Autopilot, enrolment configurations, the three update-profile kinds, macOS custom-attribute scripts, assignment filters, scope tags, device categories, terms and conditions, notification templates, app configuration both kinds, app protection for iOS/Android/Windows, WIP, app categories — each under its own read scope, consented as the union of what is CHOSEN, so picking only the five asks exactly what v0.1 asked. EVERY FILE CARRIES A SHA-256 in the manifest and THE MANIFEST GOES INTO THE ZIP LAST, TenuVault's atomicity rule kept exactly: its presence marks the backup complete. VERIFY RUNS OFFLINE in the tab — no sign-in — naming modified, missing and untracked files; an archive without a manifest is called a backup that never finished; a pre-checksum archive is UNVERIFIABLE, never quietly passed. ASSIGNMENTS EXPORT AND IMPORT (Mihai's amendment to the R15 card, which had said read-only): assignments.json carries every assigned policy's targets serialised through T11's cleaner — one implementation of what an assignment is — and imports back under three hard rules: SAME TENANT ONLY (the archive carries the tenant id, a mismatch is refused with no override, an identity-less older archive gates on typing the domain), ALL-OR-NOTHING PER POLICY when an archived list names a since-deleted group (writing the list without it changes its meaning; a dropped exclusion widens), and T11'S WRITE DISCIPLINE WHOLE — same policy ids never a namesake, four surfaces under the held write scope, dry run, fresh-read drift skip, read-back verify, sequential, never retried, the write scope asked at the click. Import is refused outright for a tampered-or-damaged archive. groups.json resolves every targeted group to its name AT BACKUP TIME, for the audit question the directory can no longer answer. Original v0.1/0.2 note: BETA \u2014 v0.2 (R09, after Ugur Koc's Restore Intune Configuration + two TenuVault rules): THE OTHER DIRECTION. Load an archive \u2014 this tool's or the original script's, the folder layout is shared and load-bearing \u2014 pick objects, dry-run, apply. CREATE ONLY: nothing of yours is patched or deleted; the one exception is TenuVault's, kept exactly \u2014 an ADMX template THIS restore half-created and failed to finish is rolled back, its own mess and nothing else. Every object arrives under an editable [Restored] prefix and UNASSIGNED (shipped narrower than the card's off-by-default assignment toggle, on purpose: a source-tenant group GUID either does not exist in the target or names a different group). THE COLLISION STOP: a name that exists in the tenant skips that object \u2014 checked at dry run AND re-checked fresh immediately before each create, because the tenant can change in between; overwrite is DELIBERATELY NOT OFFERED (a departure from the card's first draft, said on the card \u2014 overwrite is a patch wearing a restore's clothes). The dry run lists every operation INCLUDING the ADMX definition-value child writes the original's -WhatIf never shows; settings-catalog settings ride the create inline; compliance policies carry their scheduledActionsForRule with source-tenant ids stripped; ADMX definition binds go to Microsoft's own definition ids, which are the same in every tenant. Non-restorable files (script without a body) cannot be selected and say why. Created is the tenant's word: every create is verified by read-back. Scopes at the apply click: the config write for four areas, the scripts write for platform scripts \u2014 both already declared. \u2014 the configuration surface read out and written to one .zip in the browser: device configuration profiles, settings catalog policies WITH their settings, compliance policies with their scheduled actions, administrative templates with their definition values, and platform scripts (Windows PowerShell and macOS shell) with their bodies. After Ugur Koc's backup-intune-configuration.ps1, and THE FOLDER LAYOUT AND FILE NAMES ARE HIS EXACTLY \u2014 an archive written here restores with his script, one written by his script reads back here. That interoperability is worth more than any tidier scheme. Reads only, and one scope: DeviceManagementConfiguration.Read.All. THE N+1 IS THE WHOLE COST: three of the five areas need a second request per object because the list endpoint returns a count or a null where the content should be, and the original does those in series with a 100ms sleep, which on 400 policies is over a minute of a browser doing nothing. Bounded concurrency here, with errors captured per item. AN OBJECT WHOSE DETAIL COULD NOT BE READ IS LEFT OUT OF THE ARCHIVE rather than written without it \u2014 a settings-catalog policy restored with the right name and no settings is worse than one visibly missing. The manifest keeps the original's four fields so the shape stays familiar, and adds what it lacks: an index of every file with its type, Graph surface and whether it is restorable; the tenant it came from, because the question six months later is 'is this the right tenant'; and an explicit record of what the archive CANNOT restore. Two of those are stated up front rather than discovered at restore: Graph never returns secret values, so encrypted OMA-URI settings, passwords and certificates are references that must be re-entered; and a script saved without its body cannot be put back, which the original's -SkipScriptContent switch produces silently. An area that could not be read at all marks the whole archive PARTIAL. After Ugur Koc's Backup Intune Configuration (MIT)" },
  toolAudit: { t: 3, v: "1.0.2", note: "BETA \u2014 who changed Intune, what moved, and when. v0.2 (build 10413, Option A of a two-option mockup round) rebuilds the SCREEN without touching what is read: FOUR STAT CARDS answer the first questions before any scrolling \u2014 total changes, most active admin, most active area, failure rate, each computed from the rows currently shown so the filters move them too, and null on an empty window rather than a placeholder; A TIMELINE with an operation dot and badge per event \u2014 created, deleted, updated, assigned, action \u2014 classified from Graph's own activityOperationType with the activity text as fallback, coloured from the house tokens so the dark theme needs no second rule set; CLICK A CARD AND THE DETAIL UNFOLDS IN PLACE \u2014 actor with IP and kind, result and severity, correlation id, resource ids, and the full field-level diff \u2014 with the open set keyed on EVENT IDS so a filter change redraws the list and keeps your cards open; A RANGE PICKER with the presets plus a CUSTOM day range, where the from is clamped to the 30-day retention floor AND THE CLAMP IS REPORTED \u2014 a range that quietly shrank would read as a quiet tenant \u2014 and a picked day means the whole day, to 23:59:59Z, because half-open ranges off a date picker are how 'up to Tuesday' silently excludes Tuesday; and A FILTER DIALOG holding the same controls with the same wildcard semantics, plus operation type and an actor dropdown \u2014 category, operation and actor lists all built from what the tenant actually emitted, the rule the tool has had since 10323. Everything is DOM in this tab; the dialog and popover are divs, not portals, and filters still run over rows already read. One tool over deviceManagement/auditEvents with two views, because both are one Graph read and two tiles would be two copies of the same paging, throttling and export. POLICY CHANGES after Ugur Koc's check-policy-changes.ps1: configuration activity with a severity read off the activity type \u2014 a delete or a failure outranks a create, a create outranks an assignment change. ALL EVENTS after his get-intune-audit-logs.ps1: every category, filtered by actor, activity, category and result, with wildcards honoured as wildcards rather than quietly treated as substrings. THE DIFF IS ENCA'S T16, PORTED, and it is the reason to use this over the portal: modifiedProperties values arrive as JSON strings, sometimes double-encoded, sometimes wrapped in a one-element array, and the useful answer is a field-level list of what moved \u2014 assignment lists compared as sets so you see added and removed rather than two blobs. Both originals print one before/after pair and stop, which for a settings-catalog policy means several kilobytes of JSON in a CSV cell. THREE UPSTREAM BUGS FIXED: the two scripts window the same endpoint two ways and a bare date means midnight, so 'the last day' was anything from a minute to 24 hours \u2014 full ISO-8601 UTC in both; the fixed category list contains values Graph never emits, so choosing one returned nothing and read as a quiet tenant \u2014 categories come from the tenant's own data; and the policy view is not capped at five rows after paging everything. Every resource on a record is inspected rather than the first, because an assignment change puts the policy in slot 0 and the group in slot 1. Windows stop at 30 days because that is Intune's audit retention, and the screen says so rather than offering a 90-day option that returns a confidently empty answer. Reads only. After Ugur Koc's two monitoring scripts (MIT) and ENCA's T16" },
  toolRoles: { t: 7, v: "1.0.4", note: "BETA \u2014 v0.4 (build 10471): THE ROLE OPENS TO WHAT IT ALLOWS \u2014 a \u2699 permissions button on every role head, the portal\u0027s per-category grid on one page in the same modal: the run\u0027s $select drops rolePermissions deliberately (large, rarely asked), so ONE definition is re-read on the click under the RBAC scope already in hand; actions parsed as Microsoft.Intune_Category_Action into the portal\u0027s own grouping, allowed as inc chips, the rare notAllowed shown as DENIED rather than silently subtracted, and the allow-list sentence stated \u2014 anything not named is not granted. Cached per role per run; exports untouched. v0.3 (build 10470): A GROUP MEMBER ANSWERS WHO IS IN IT \u2014 the report keeps showing the assignment AS WRITTEN, group as group, because whoever can change its membership can grant the role without holding it; the \ud83d\udc65 members button opens ENCA\u0027s modal with the NEXT answer, read live on the click and never during the run: transitiveMembers/microsoft.graph.user so nesting is flattened to who EFFECTIVELY holds the role, users only (a device or service principal in the group holds no Intune RBAC), ENCA\u0027s 500 cap with the true total worn, disabled accounts tagged, cached per group per run, no new scope \u2014 the directory scopes already asked cover the expansion. v0.2 (build 10392): RENAMED to Intune RBAC \u2014 the tool is the RBAC surface, not one question about it. Role assignments becomes its FIRST SECTION, and the section seg is where scope tags, custom-role review and whatever else belongs to Intune RBAC will land \u2014 each as a section here rather than a new tile. One tool per surface, sections per question. Everything below is the Role assignments section, unchanged. \u2014 who can change Intune, on one page. Every role definition, built-in and custom, each assignment under it, the users and groups in it resolved to names, its scope tags and what it is scoped over. After Ugur Koc's get-intune-role-assignments.ps1 (MIT), whose question this is; the portal makes you click into one role at a time, which is why nobody audits this. NAMES ARE RESOLVED IN ONE CALL RATHER THAN TWO PER MEMBER: the original probes /users/{id} and then /groups/{id} for each principal, which on a surface where a hundred members is ordinary is up to two hundred round trips; directoryObjects/getByIds answers a thousand at once AND says what type each object is, so there is nothing to probe. TWO THINGS THE ORIGINAL DOES NOT READ, both of which change the answer: scope tags, which are how a tenant gives a regional helpdesk a role over only its own devices and whose absence overstates every scoped assignment; and the scope itself, read from scopeType and resourceScopes together, so that 'all devices' and 'Graph did not say' are different answers here \u2014 the original falls back to the literal string All when resourceScopes is empty, which is right often enough to be dangerous. EMPTY ROLES ARE A TOGGLE, not a hidden row: a role with nobody in it is one membership change from being a live grant, and switching it on re-renders what was already read rather than asking the tenant again. CONCENTRATIONS ARE OBSERVATIONS AND THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO SCORE \u2014 an assignment scoped to all devices, a broad built-in role with a lot of members at a threshold the screen states because it is arbitrary, a role whose members are all one group where whoever can change that group can grant the role without holding it. And the sentence neither the script nor the portal says, carried on the screen and in all three exports: THIS IS INTUNE RBAC ONLY. Global Administrator and Intune Administrator are Entra directory roles, they grant full access to Intune, and they do not appear in deviceManagement/roleAssignments \u2014 an access review that takes this for the whole list is missing the accounts with the most access. An assignment whose role definition could not be resolved is kept under a role that says so rather than dropped, because dropping it takes a live grant off the review. Exports Markdown, CSV one row per member, and a standalone HTML report. After Ugur Koc's Get Intune Role Assignments (MIT)" },
  toolDevice: { t: 6, v: "1.0.2", note: "BETA \u2014 R04's assignment graph walked backwards from one device. Find a machine by name, serial, Intune device id or Entra device id \u2014 every device has two GUIDs and which one is on the ticket is a coin toss, so both are tried \u2014 OR BY ITS PRIMARY USER, the thing the ticket actually names: a term with an @ is tried as a UPN before the device filters, an exact display name matches in the inventory scan, and a GUID gets a third try as the user's object id, all off fields the enrolment record already carries (userId, userPrincipalName, userDisplayName in LIST_SELECT) so NO NEW SCOPE is asked. A MULTI-MATCH IS A PICK, NOT AN ERROR \u2014 a user with a laptop and a phone is the everyday case \u2014 rendered as T19's clickable .scard cards with the primary user, compliance and LAST CHECK-IN on the card, because which of these is the live one is usually the whole question; a click analyzes that device and the report's matched-on line says the route and that it was picked from N. The go-find-the-GUID dead end is gone from the name, serial and Entra-id collision paths as a side effect \u2014 and see which of its own Entra groups, which of its primary user's groups, or which tenant-wide target put each policy there. THE ENDPOINT LIST IS NOT A SECOND COPY: T06 builds a match set and hands it to T02's own source table, because the first time a surface was added to one and not the other this tool would have started reporting a device as unaffected by a workload it never looked at, with nothing on screen to say so. TENANT-WIDE TARGETS ARE ON BY DEFAULT, which is the real difference from T02 rather than a preference \u2014 for a group, All Devices is the same answer every time and says nothing about the group you asked about; for a device it IS the answer, and hiding it would hide policy the machine is definitely getting. All Users lands on the person, so a device with no primary user does not receive it, and the row says that rather than being dropped. INTENDED AND ACTUAL ARE TWO COLUMNS and the gap between them is the point: everything from an assignment is what Intune intends, what the machine has is whatever it collected at its last check-in, so the check-in time is the first thing on the page and the report states that a policy assigned minutes ago has not applied. deviceConfigurationStates and deviceCompliancePolicyStates supply the device's own verdict where they answer; where they 403, come back empty, or cannot be tied to a policy with certainty \u2014 two policies sharing a name is enough \u2014 the state is unknown, never fine by omission. A FILTERED ASSIGNMENT IS AN OPEN QUESTION: the rule is evaluated by the service against inventory a browser cannot see and Graph has no call that answers it, so those rows say may reach it and name the filter. An exclusion beats an inclusion, except where the two arrive through different kinds of group \u2014 device against user \u2014 where whether it applies depends on how the policy is targeted and the assignment does not say; that is called a conflict rather than guessed. Adds one scope, Device.Read.All, because the Entra device object is not the Intune enrolment record. Exports Markdown, CSV and a standalone HTML report" },
  toolGroupUse: { t: 2, v: "1.0.4", note: "BETA — v0.4 (R19, after Alper Atar's IntuneShade, MIT): the sweep gains a second face — the ASSIGNMENT MATRIX, groups down the side, surfaces across the top, rendered from the sweep's one read with a Table/Matrix switch that asks the tenant nothing. A cell is a claim and the grid does not overstate it: includes are the count, exclusions their own red −n per surface (never folded in — 'does not reach you' drawn as coverage is the overstatement a matrix invites), dangling references wear the deleted flag instead of counting, and the EMPTY-GROUP CHECK is opt-in and absent-not-empty: one $top=1 transitive-members probe per group batched twenty a trip (T09's technique) behind a button that names its cost; a failed probe is 'reach?', unknown, never empty. A group click lands in single-group mode through the tile's own pipeline. — the first TUNO tool that reads a tenant. Name an Entra group and get everything Intune sends to its members across nine surfaces and twenty Graph endpoints: configuration profiles (device, settings catalog, ADMX), compliance, scripts and remediations (PowerShell, macOS shell, remediation), application assignments, app protection, app configuration, enrolment restrictions, Autopilot and Windows update profiles. Two parents, and the merge is the point. ENCA's Group Analyzer (T19) supplies the machinery — the source descriptor with its own scopes and runner, the flat hit shape, the rule that a failing source is reported and never fatal, and ancestors rather than just the group itself. Ugur Koc's Get Group Assignments supplies the Intune surface map and two things ENCA's version does not do: EXCLUSIONS ARE READ AS ASSIGNMENTS, because 'this policy does not reach you' is the same question as 'this policy reaches you' and a report showing only inclusions is wrong half the time; and TENANT-WIDE TARGETS ARE OFFERED as a toggle, because All Users and All Devices land on the group's members too and the effective surface is larger than the group's own assignments. And one thing neither has: the script's own notes say nested group inheritance is not evaluated, so an inherited assignment is included here and says which parent it came through. A surface that could not be read is never reported as empty — it is reported as unknown, with the permission and the Intune RBAC role it would need, because those are different things. Intune only, deliberately: where a group is used in Conditional Access, licensing, directory roles or access packages needs scopes TUNO has no reason to hold, and the tool points at ENCA rather than half-reading them. Exports Markdown, CSV and a standalone HTML report that opens for someone with no access to the tenant. v0.2 adds ENCA's TENANT SWEEP, with the Conditional Access scope replaced by its Intune equivalent. A sweep reads each surface ONCE and matches every group against it, so the cost barely grows with the group count — 300 groups is still twenty reads. 'Only groups Intune assigns to' takes the ids off the assignments as they are read, so there is no /groups enumeration at all; it is the fastest scope and it CANNOT find unused groups, which the screen says where the choice is made rather than leaving an empty list to be misread as a clean bill of health. The counted scopes enumerate and can. A name filter narrows the scan server-side where Graph supports the shape, falling back to listing and filtering locally — and the local check is always the final word, because $search matches tokens rather than substrings and returns more than it should. Group nesting is off by default and batched twenty at a time when on; with it off a group that only receives policy through a parent reads as zero, and the report says so rather than letting it pass as unused. An id an assignment names that the directory no longer has is kept and flagged as a DANGLING REFERENCE: that assignment targets nobody and nothing in the portal will tell you. After Ugur Koc's Get Group Assignments (MIT) and ENCA's T19" },
};
